window.HIA_TOPICS=[{"id": "ai", "name": "Edge AI & Vision"}, {"id": "monitor", "name": "Monitoring & Energy"}, {"id": "control", "name": "Automation & Control"}, {"id": "rpi", "name": "Raspberry Pi in Production"}, {"id": "events", "name": "Workshops & Events"}];
window.HIA=[{"slug": "how-raspberry-pi-reads-industrial-sensors", "title": "How Raspberry Pi Reads Data from Industrial Sensors", "date": "02 Nov 2026", "author": "Khairul Tajudin", "topic": "control", "kind": "Explainer", "desc": "Learn how Raspberry Pi reads industrial sensors with Modbus and RS485, and how the Cytron Industrial IO HAT+ adds RS485, RS232 and isolated I/O", "cover": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/tutorial/how-raspberry-pi-reads-industrial-sensors/how-raspberry-pi-reads-data-from-industrial-sensors-320x180.png", "hero": "https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/how-raspberry-pi-reads-industrial-sensors/how-raspberry-pi-reads-data-from-industrial-sensors.png", "tags": ["Raspberry Pi 5", "Industrial IO HAT+", "Modbus", "RS485", "RS232", "Industrial Sensor"], "products": [{"name": "Industrial Grade RS485 Temperature & Humidity Sensor", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-industrial-grade-rs485-temperature-humidity-sensor", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/SN-HUTE-RS485/SN-HUTE-RS485-a-268x268.jpg", "price": "RM179.00", "old": null}, {"name": "Raspberry Pi 5 Single Board Computer", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-raspberry-pi-5", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/V-RASPBERRY-PI-5/v-raspberry-pi-5-main-268x268.jpg", "price": "RM202.00++", "old": null}, {"name": "Industrial IO HAT+ for Raspberry Pi 5 and Argon Industria Case", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-industry-hat-plus-for-raspberry-pi-5", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/HAT-IND-AG/hat-ind-ag-a-268x268.png", "price": "RM138.60", "old": null}, {"name": "USB to 2CH RS485 Isolated Converter", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-usb-to-2ch-rs485-isolated-converter", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/CA-USB-2CH-485/usb-to-2ch-rs485-1-1-268x268.png", "price": "RM89.00", "old": null}], "words": 477, "useCase": false, "body": "<h2>Introduction</h2><p>Factories monitor their machines and processes on SCADA screens, and the data on those screens comes from industrial sensors. So how does a <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-raspberry-pi-5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Raspberry Pi</a> read data from an industrial sensor?</p><h2>Raspberry Pi Can Read Modbus</h2><p>The answer is <strong>Modbus</strong>. Raspberry Pi can read Modbus, the same protocol used by sensors such as this <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-industrial-grade-rs485-temperature-humidity-sensor\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">industrial-grade temperature and humidity sensor</a>.</p><p><img alt=\"Industrial grade RS485 temperature and humidity sensor with Modbus output\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/how-raspberry-pi-reads-industrial-sensors/02-industrial-rs485-temperature-humidity-sensor.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>What is Modbus?</h2><p>Modbus is the <strong>standard language for industrial sensors</strong>. It connects devices such as SCADA systems, PLCs, drives, power meters, valves and loop controllers.</p><p><img alt=\"Modbus network connecting SCADA, PLC, drives, valves and controllers\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/how-raspberry-pi-reads-industrial-sensors/03-what-is-modbus-industrial-network.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p>All the data from a Modbus device comes out in <strong>register addresses</strong>. Each type of data has its own address range:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Address Range</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Read Coil (Output) Status</td><td>0xxxx</td></tr><tr><td>Read Holding Registers</td><td>4xxxx</td></tr><tr><td>Read Input Registers</td><td>3xxxx</td></tr><tr><td>Force Single Coil (Output)</td><td>0xxxx</td></tr><tr><td>Preset Single Register</td><td>4xxxx</td></tr><tr><td>Force Multiple Coils (Outputs)</td><td>0xxxx</td></tr><tr><td>Preset Multiple Registers</td><td>4xxxx</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>How Raspberry Pi Reads a Sensor Value</h2><p>For example, a sensor gives its value in register <strong>40002</strong>. Raspberry Pi pulls the data from that register and decodes it into a reading, such as <strong>38°C</strong>. The same works for other values such as pressure.</p><p><strong>Sensor → Register 40002 → Raspberry Pi → 38°C</strong></p><p><img alt=\"Sensor value in Modbus register 40002 decoded by Raspberry Pi as 38°C\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/how-raspberry-pi-reads-industrial-sensors/05-modbus-register-40002-raspberry-pi-38c.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>RS485: The Key to Connecting Industrial Sensors</h2><p>To connect industrial sensors, Raspberry Pi must have <strong>RS485</strong>, for example through a <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-usb-to-2ch-rs485-isolated-converter\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">USB to 2CH RS485 Isolated Converter</a>.</p><p><img alt=\"RS485 module used to connect Raspberry Pi to industrial sensors\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/how-raspberry-pi-reads-industrial-sensors/06-usb-to-2ch-rs485-isolated-converter.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p>The wiring is simple. RS485 uses only <strong>2 wires</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>A</strong> to <strong>A</strong></li><li><strong>B</strong> to <strong>B</strong></li></ul><p>After that, power the sensor and Raspberry Pi can read it straight away. In the example below, a Raspberry Pi connects through an RS485 module to a turbidity sensor.</p><p><strong>Raspberry Pi → RS485 module → A/B wires → Industrial sensor</strong></p><p><img alt=\"Wiring diagram of Raspberry Pi, RS485 module and turbidity sensor\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/how-raspberry-pi-reads-industrial-sensors/07-raspberry-pi-rs485-sensor-wiring-diagram.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>Cytron Industrial IO HAT+</h2><p>For a setup, we usually use the <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-industry-hat-plus-for-raspberry-pi-5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Cytron Industrial IO HAT+</a>, because it has RS485, RS232, isolated digital I/O and more. It is built for industrial environments.</p><p><img alt=\"Cytron Industrial IO HAT+ for Raspberry Pi 5\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/how-raspberry-pi-reads-industrial-sensors/08-cytron-industrial-io-hat-plus.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p>Its industrial interfaces:</p><ul><li><strong>1x RS485</strong>: up to 1 Mbps with automatic direction control, mapped to UART0 (TX: GPIO14, RX: GPIO15)</li><li><strong>1x RS232</strong>: up to 250 kbps, mapped to UART4 (TX: GPIO12, RX: GPIO13)</li><li><strong>6x isolated digital inputs</strong>: bidirectional, for PNP or NPN sensors up to 50V</li><li><strong>4x isolated digital outputs</strong>: dry contact solid-state relays, DC or AC loads up to 50V 0.5A</li><li><strong>Wide input voltage</strong>: DC 10–30V, surge protected</li></ul><p><img alt=\"Industrial IO HAT+ features\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/how-raspberry-pi-reads-industrial-sensors/09a-industrial-io-hat-plus-features.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p><img alt=\"pinout with RS485, RS232 and isolated I/O\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/how-raspberry-pi-reads-industrial-sensors/09b-industrial-io-hat-plus-pinout.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>Raspberry Pi reads industrial sensors through Modbus: the sensor puts its value in a register, and Raspberry Pi pulls and decodes it into a reading such as 38°C. To connect, all it needs is RS485 with two wires, A to A and B to B, and the <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-industry-hat-plus-for-raspberry-pi-5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Industrial IO HAT+</a> adds RS485, RS232 and isolated I/O built for industrial environments.</p><p>New to industrial monitoring? Read <a href=\"design-h-tutorial.html?slug=what-is-scada-a-simple-introduction-to-industrial-monitoring\">What is SCADA? A Simple Introduction to Industrial Monitoring</a>.</p><p>Have a question? Leave it in the comments in our Telegram Group.</p>"}, {"slug": "why-factories-are-switching-to-raspberry-pi", "title": "Why Factories Are Switching to Raspberry Pi", "date": "25 Oct 2026", "author": "Khairul Tajudin", "topic": "rpi", "kind": "Insight", "desc": "Learn why Domino’s, Formlabs, SENECA and KwickPOS use Raspberry Pi: stable, easy to maintain, customisable, flexible and open source.", "cover": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/tutorial/why-factories-are-switching-to-raspberry-pi/why-factories-are-switching-to-raspberry-pi-320x180.png", "hero": "https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/why-factories-are-switching-to-raspberry-pi/why-factories-are-switching-to-raspberry-pi.png", "tags": ["Raspberry Pi", "IRIV", "RP2040", "Compute Module", "Industrial IoT", "Factory Automation"], "products": [{"name": "IRIV PiControl - IR4.0 Industrial Controller", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/V-IRIV-PICONTROL/v-iriv-picontrol-main-3-268x268.jpg", "price": "RM1,489.00++", "old": null}, {"name": "Raspberry Pi 5 Single Board Computer", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-raspberry-pi-5", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/V-RASPBERRY-PI-5/v-raspberry-pi-5-main-268x268.jpg", "price": "RM202.00++", "old": null}, {"name": "IRIV SmartHub CM5", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-smarthub-cm5", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/V-IRIV-SH5/iriv-smarthub-cm5-with-wifi-antenna-view-7-min-268x268.png", "price": "RM925.00++", "old": null}, {"name": "IRIV EdgeAI CM5 - IR4.0 Industrial AI Controller", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/V-IRIV-EAI5/iriv-edgeai-cm5-view-1-1-268x268.png", "price": "RM2,487.00++", "old": null}], "words": 316, "useCase": false, "body": "<h2>Introduction</h2><p>Many big factories have started using a small computer you may already know: the <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/c-raspberry-pi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Raspberry Pi</a>.</p><p>Raspberry Pi is now used more and more widely, especially in the industrial sector. Here are four companies that use it, and why they chose it.</p><h2>Raspberry Pi in Industry: Four Examples</h2><h3>Domino's: Digital Signage and Self-Ordering</h3><p>Domino's uses Raspberry Pi for its <strong>digital signage</strong> and <strong>self-ordering</strong>, through Raspberry Pi-powered signage from Yodeck. Read more in <a href=\"design-h-industry-article.html?slug=what-is-digital-signage-raspberry-pi\">What is Digital Signage and How Does It Work?</a>.</p><p><img alt=\"Raspberry Pi-powered digital signage from Yodeck used by Domino's\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/why-factories-are-switching-to-raspberry-pi/01-yodeck-raspberry-pi-digital-signage.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h3>Formlabs: 3D Printer Control</h3><p>Formlabs uses Raspberry Pi in its 3D printers to control the <strong>firmware</strong> and the <strong>printer interface</strong>.</p><p><img alt=\"Formlabs 3D printer with a touchscreen interface controlled by Raspberry Pi\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/why-factories-are-switching-to-raspberry-pi/02-formlabs-3d-printer-raspberry-pi.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h3>SENECA: Factory Automation</h3><p>SENECA uses Raspberry Pi technology (the RP2040 chip) for factory automation. Its industrial-grade I/O modules <strong>connect sensors</strong>, <strong>read data</strong> and <strong>control machines</strong>, gathering accurate data from temperature sensors.</p><p><img alt=\"SENECA industrial I/O module powered by the Raspberry Pi RP2040 chip\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/why-factories-are-switching-to-raspberry-pi/03-seneca-io-modules-raspberry-pi-rp2040.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h3>KwickPOS: Point of Sale</h3><p>KwickPOS uses Raspberry Pi in its point-of-sale systems for busy restaurants and retail stores.</p><p><img alt=\"KwickPOS point-of-sale system built on a Raspberry Pi Compute Module\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/why-factories-are-switching-to-raspberry-pi/04-kwickpos-raspberry-pi-point-of-sale.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p>It runs fully on Linux, with Wi-Fi and HDMI, which is enough to operate a retail business.</p><p><img alt=\"KwickPOS Raspberry Pi unit with fully Linux, Wi-Fi and HDMI\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/why-factories-are-switching-to-raspberry-pi/05-kwickpos-fully-linux-wifi-hdmi.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>Why Factories Choose Raspberry Pi</h2><p>That is why many big companies now choose Raspberry Pi:</p><ul><li><strong>Stable</strong>: reliable enough for daily operations.</li><li><strong>Easy to maintain</strong>: simple to keep running.</li><li><strong>Customisable</strong>: can be adapted to each company's needs.</li></ul><p>It is not about being cheap. It is about being <strong>flexible</strong> and <strong>open source</strong>.</p><h2>Raspberry Pi for Industrial Use</h2><p>For industrial projects, Cytron's <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/IRIV-PiControl\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV</a> range brings Raspberry Pi into industrial controllers. See how other industries use it in <a href=\"design-h-industry-article.html?slug=how-big-industries-use-raspberry-pi\">How Big Industries Use Raspberry Pi Today</a>.</p><p><img alt=\"Cytron IRIV industrial controllers based on Raspberry Pi on a desk\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/why-factories-are-switching-to-raspberry-pi/iriv-picontrol-board-top-transparent.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>From Domino's digital signage and Formlabs 3D printers to SENECA factory automation and KwickPOS retail systems, Raspberry Pi has moved well into industry. Companies choose it because it is stable, easy to maintain and customisable, and above all flexible and open source.</p><p>Have a question? Leave it in the comments in our <a href=\"http://qr.link/BXAybc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Telegram Group</a>.</p>"}, {"slug": "how-big-industries-use-raspberry-pi", "title": "How Big Industries Use Raspberry Pi Today", "date": "19 Oct 2026", "author": "Khairul Tajudin", "topic": "rpi", "kind": "Insight", "desc": "Learn how Domino’s, HP and EpiSensor use Raspberry Pi in industry, and how IRIV EdgeAI CM5 brings it to industrial systems with built-in AI and RS485", "cover": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/tutorial/how-big-industries-use-raspberry-pi/how-big-industries-use-raspberry-pi-today-320x180.png", "hero": "https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/how-big-industries-use-raspberry-pi/how-big-industries-use-raspberry-pi-today.png", "tags": ["IRIV EdgeAI CM5", "Raspberry Pi", "Compute Module 4", "Compute Module 5", "Edge AI", "RS485"], "products": [{"name": "IRIV EdgeAI CM5 - IR4.0 Industrial AI Controller", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/V-IRIV-EAI5/iriv-edgeai-cm5-view-1-1-268x268.png", "price": "RM2,487.00++", "old": null}], "words": 510, "useCase": false, "body": "<h2>Introduction</h2><p>Raspberry Pi is often seen as a board for schools and hobby projects. Many people have had that experience when they first use one.</p><p>Today, the picture is different. Raspberry Pi has moved into large industries, well beyond education, and Cytron now offers it in an industrial form with the <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI CM5</a>.</p><h2>Raspberry Pi in Large Industries</h2><p>Well-known companies already run Raspberry Pi in their products and operations. Three examples stand out.</p><h3>Digital Signage at Domino's</h3><p>Domino's uses Raspberry Pi for its digital signage. Raspberry Pi describes it as a worldwide digital signage success story, built on Raspberry Pi's balance between power and cost. Read more in <a href=\"design-h-industry-article.html?slug=what-is-digital-signage-raspberry-pi\">What is Digital Signage and How Does It Work?</a>.</p><p><img alt=\"Domino's restaurant digital signage running on Raspberry Pi\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/how-big-industries-use-raspberry-pi/01-dominos-raspberry-pi-digital-signage.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"><br> </p><h3>Digital Printing at HP</h3><p>HP uses a <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-raspberry-pi-cm4-with-wireless-pick-ram-and-emmc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Raspberry Pi Compute Module 4</a> to control its digital printing press. A Compute Module 4-based panel PC monitors and controls the press, making the industrial manufacturing process easier and more efficient.</p><p><img alt=\"HP digital printing press controlled by a Raspberry Pi Compute Module 4 panel PC\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/how-big-industries-use-raspberry-pi/02-hp-digital-printing-press-compute-module-4.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"><br> </p><h3>Energy Monitoring with EpiSensor</h3><p>In the energy sector, Raspberry Pi is used for <strong>energy monitoring</strong> and <strong>IoT infrastructure</strong>. EpiSensor uses Raspberry Pi Compute Module 4 as the IoT infrastructure layer for its energy services.</p><p><img alt=\"EpiSensor energy monitoring IoT infrastructure built on Raspberry Pi Compute Module 4\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/how-big-industries-use-raspberry-pi/03-episensor-energy-monitoring-raspberry-pi.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h3>And Many More</h3><p>There are many more examples. Search for Raspberry Pi success stories and you will find large industries using it, from IoT and automation devices built on the <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-raspberry-pi-cm5-with-wireless-pick-ram-and-emmc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Raspberry Pi Compute Module 5</a> to Raspberry Pi computers replacing desktop PCs. See more in <a href=\"design-h-tutorial.html?slug=raspberry-pi-success-stories-across-industries\">Raspberry Pi Success Stories Across Industries</a>.</p><p><img alt=\"Raspberry Pi industry success stories in IoT, automation and computing\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/how-big-industries-use-raspberry-pi/04-raspberry-pi-industry-success-stories.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>Raspberry Pi for Industry: IRIV EdgeAI CM5</h2><p>To use Raspberry Pi in industry, Cytron has introduced the <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI CM5</a>, an industrial AI controller built for the job.</p><p><img alt=\"Cytron IRIV EdgeAI CM5 industrial AI controller in a metal enclosure\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/how-big-industries-use-raspberry-pi/05-iriv-edgeai-cm5-industrial-ai-controller.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p>Its key features:</p><ul><li><strong>Metal enclosure</strong>: resistant to dust and heat.</li><li><strong>Built-in AI</strong>: AI acceleration measured in TOPS.</li><li><strong>RS485</strong> and other industrial interfaces.</li></ul><h3>Built-in AI and TOPS</h3><p>TOPS (tera operations per second) measures how many trillion calculations a processor can perform each second. It shows how fast the processor can run AI tasks.</p><p>Raspberry Pi AI accelerators such as the <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-raspberry-pi-ai-hat-plus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Raspberry Pi AI HAT+</a> come in two levels:</p><ul><li><strong>13 TOPS AI accelerator</strong>: ideal for compact models, simple detection and low-power scenarios.</li><li><strong>26 TOPS AI accelerator</strong>: for high-resolution vision tasks, multi-camera processing and advanced deep learning models.</li></ul><p><img alt=\"TOPS explained with 13 TOPS and 26 TOPS Raspberry Pi AI accelerators\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/how-big-industries-use-raspberry-pi/06-ai-accelerator-13-26-tops.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h3>Industrial-Grade I/O Interfaces</h3><p>The IRIV EdgeAI CM5 comes with isolated industrial interfaces:</p><ul><li>2x isolated bidirectional digital input (up to 50V)</li><li>2x isolated bidirectional digital output (up to 50V)</li><li>1x isolated RS485</li></ul><p><img alt=\"IRIV EdgeAI CM5 isolated digital input, digital output and RS485 interfaces\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/how-big-industries-use-raspberry-pi/07-iriv-edgeai-cm5-industrial-io-interfaces.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>Not Just for Education Anymore</h2><p>Raspberry Pi is no longer only for education. It has become the backbone of <strong>smart and scalable</strong> industrial systems.</p><h2>Hands-On Tutorial</h2><p>To start with the IRIV EdgeAI CM5, follow <a href=\"design-h-tutorial.html?slug=getting-started-with-iriv-edgeai-cm5\">Getting Started with Cytron IRIV EdgeAI CM5</a>.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>From Domino's digital signage to HP's digital printing press and EpiSensor's energy monitoring, Raspberry Pi now runs in large industries, not only in classrooms. With the IRIV EdgeAI CM5, it comes in a metal enclosure with built-in AI and industrial interfaces such as RS485, ready for smart and scalable industrial systems.</p><p>Have a question? Leave it in the comments in our Telegram Group.</p>"}, {"slug": "what-is-digital-signage-raspberry-pi", "title": "What is Digital Signage and How Does It Work?", "date": "14 Oct 2026", "author": "Khairul Tajudin", "topic": "rpi", "kind": "Explainer", "desc": "Learn how digital signage runs screens in malls and restaurants 24/7, and how to build one with Raspberry Pi and IRIV SmartHub CM5", "cover": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/tutorial/what-is-digital-signage-raspberry-pi/what-is-digital-signage-and-how-does-it-work-320x180.png", "hero": "https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/what-is-digital-signage-raspberry-pi/what-is-digital-signage-and-how-does-it-work.png", "tags": ["IRIV SmartHub", "SmartHub CM5", "Raspberry Pi 5", "digital signage", "Cloud Management", "Retail"], "products": [{"name": "IRIV SmartHub CM5", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-smarthub-cm5", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/V-IRIV-SH5/iriv-smarthub-cm5-with-wifi-antenna-view-7-min-268x268.png", "price": "RM925.00++", "old": null}], "words": 449, "useCase": false, "body": "<h2>Introduction</h2><p>Screens that play video advertisements or display food menus are everywhere today, from shopping malls to F&amp;B restaurants such as McDonald's and KFC. Many people see them every day without thinking about what keeps them running.</p><p>The system behind these screens is called digital signage. In many installations, a small computer such as the <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-raspberry-pi-5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Raspberry Pi 5</a> sits behind the screen and controls everything that is shown.</p><h2>What is Digital Signage?</h2><p>Digital signage is a system that displays videos, pictures or any other information on a screen, <strong>24 hours a day, non-stop</strong>.</p><p>Common examples include:</p><ul><li>Video advertisements in shopping malls</li><li>Food menu boards in restaurants</li><li>Information screens in F&amp;B outlets</li></ul><p><img alt=\"Large digital signage screen showing a property advertisement inside a shopping mall\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/what-is-digital-signage-raspberry-pi/01-digital-signage-screen-shopping-mall.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>How Does Digital Signage Work?</h2><p>Behind the screen, there is usually one small computer. This computer controls all the videos, pictures and information shown on the display. The content can also be scheduled, so the screen shows the right content at the right time without anyone having to change it by hand.</p><p>The basic system flow is:</p><p><strong>Content (videos, pictures, information) → Small computer behind the screen → Display screen</strong></p><p><img alt=\"Display screen connected to a Raspberry Pi single-board computer for digital signage\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/what-is-digital-signage-raspberry-pi/02-digital-signage-screen-and-small-computer.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>Why Use Raspberry Pi for Digital Signage?</h2><p>Today, many digital signage systems use Raspberry Pi as the computer behind the screen. Two reasons stand out:</p><ul><li><strong>Low power consumption</strong>: it saves electricity, which matters for a screen that is always on.</li><li><strong>Continuous operation</strong>: it can run 24 hours a day without problems.</li></ul><h2>Manage Every Screen from the Cloud</h2><p>All the videos and content shown on the screens can be managed through the cloud. There is no need to walk to each TV to change its video. Content on every screen can be updated at the same time, from one place.</p><h2>Real Example: Domino's</h2><p>One of the best-known examples is Domino's, which uses Raspberry Pi for its digital signage. Raspberry Pi describes it as a worldwide digital signage success story, built on Raspberry Pi's balance between power and cost.</p><p><img alt=\"Domino's restaurant menu screens running on Raspberry Pi digital signage\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/what-is-digital-signage-raspberry-pi/03-dominos-raspberry-pi-digital-signage.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>Build Your Own Digital Signage</h2><p>Cytron provides both the hardware and the tutorials to build a digital signage system. Two hands-on tutorials are available:</p><ul><li><a href=\"design-h-tutorial.html?slug=iriv-smarthub-digital-signage\">Digital Signage With IRIV SmartHub CM5</a>: set up digital signage on the <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-smarthub-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV SmartHub CM5</a>.</li><li><a href=\"design-h-tutorial.html?slug=pisignage-raspberrypi\">Set Up piSignage on Raspberry Pi for Digital Signage</a>: set up piSignage on a Raspberry Pi.</li></ul><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>Digital signage keeps videos, menus and information running on screens 24 hours a day, controlled by a small computer behind each display. With Raspberry Pi, the system uses little power, runs continuously and lets content on every screen be updated together from the cloud.</p><p>To build one, start with the <a href=\"design-h-tutorial.html?slug=iriv-smarthub-digital-signage\">Digital Signage With IRIV SmartHub CM5</a> tutorial. Have a question? Leave it in the comments in our Telegram Group</p>"}, {"slug": "iriv-edgeai-safety-box-ready-for-oshmp30", "title": "IRIV EdgeAI Safety Box: Ready for OSHMP30", "date": "12 Oct 2026", "author": "Asri Ruslan", "topic": "ai", "kind": "Insight", "desc": "Discover the IRIV EdgeAI Safety Box – OSHMP30 for AI-powered PPE detection, hazard monitoring, and smarter workplace safety.", "cover": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-safety-box-ready-for-oshmp30/iriv-edge-ai-safety-box-01-320x180.png", "hero": "https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-safety-box-ready-for-oshmp30/iriv-edge-ai-safety-box-01.png", "tags": ["IRIV", "Edge AI", "Raspberry Pi In Industry", "CM5", "AI Vision", "Image Processing"], "products": [{"name": "IRIV EdgeAI - Safety Box: PPE and Zone Monitoring", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edgeai-safety-box-kit", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/KIT-IRIV-EAI-PPE/safety-box-b-268x268.png", "price": "RM7,999.00", "old": null}], "words": 4076, "useCase": false, "body": "<h2>Introduction</h2><p>Malaysia's new national plan for safety and health at work asks every workplace to use technology to watch for danger and manage risk. We built the IRIV EdgeAI Safety Box for that job. It turns the security cameras you already have into a round-the-clock lookout for missing helmets and safety vests, and for people or vehicles going where they shouldn't. It also keeps the proof and the numbers the plan asks you to act on.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-safety-box-ready-for-oshmp30/screenshot-2026-10-12-102432.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p><br> <b>Figure 1. Live View.</b> Each camera's picture is shown live, with a box drawn around every PPE violation or Zone intrusion&nbsp;the Safety Box spots. When a safety rule is broken, that camera's picture turns red and shows ALERT, and the list on the right shows what just happened, with a photo and a short video.</p><p>On 28 July 2026, the Department of Occupational Safety and Health (DOSH, also known as JKKP) launched Malaysia's National Occupational Safety and Health Master Plan 2026–2030, known as OSHMP30.<a href=\"https://x.com/doshmalaysia/status/2076839865560584282\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><sup>4</sup></a> For the next five years, it will shape how employers are guided, helped and checked on keeping their workers safe and healthy. One of its six main priorities is dedicated to technology.</p><p>At Cytron, we have studied the plan closely. This article explains in plain terms what OSHMP30 asks of employers, and how the IRIV EdgeAI Safety Box, developed by our team in Malaysia, helps you deliver it at your own workplace, using your own cameras.<br> <br> <img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-safety-box-ready-for-oshmp30/oshmp30-bi.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>OSHMP30 at a glance</h2><p>OSHMP30 is Malaysia's five-year plan for safety and health at work. DOSH wrote it with the Ministry of Human Resources (KESUMA) and the National Council for Occupational Safety and Health (NCOSH). It follows the previous plan, OSHMP25. It was first announced in Klang on 6 November 2025 and officially launched at the end of National OSH Week on 28 July 2026.<sup><a href=\"https://berita.rtm.gov.my/nasional/senarai-berita-nasional/senarai-artikel/pelan-induk-oshmp30-fokus-pendigitalan-daya-tahan-sistem-keselamatan-pekerjaan/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">3</a>,<a href=\"https://x.com/doshmalaysia/status/2076839865560584282\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">4</a></sup></p><p>Its theme is <i>Building a Resilient and Inclusive OSH Environment for the Future Workforce</i>. The plan is built around six main priorities, which it calls Strategic Thrusts, carried out through 22 programmes. It is meant to be delivered by government, employers and workers together.<sup>1</sup></p><table><thead><tr><th>Strategic Thrust</th><th>What it is about</th><th>Programmes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Advancing OSH Governance Strategically</td><td>Safety laws and how they are enforced, companies checking themselves, government agencies working together, safe practices at work, and road safety for people who drive for work</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>2. Championing Worker Health and Well-being</td><td>Health services for workers, designing hazards out from the start, and new health risks</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>3. Driving OSH Innovation and Technology Integration</td><td>To promote the integration of innovative technologies and digital solutions in OSH practices to enhance safety monitoring and risk management across various sectors.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>4. Reinvigorating MSMEs and High-Risk Sectors</td><td>Support and incentives for small businesses and for the industries where most accidents happen</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>5. Shaping the Future of a Dynamic Workplace</td><td>Gig and app-based work, working from home, and workplaces that can cope with disruption</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>6. Cultivating OSH Culture through Progressive Capacity Development</td><td>Safety in schools and universities, research, and training standards</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>The 2030 targets</h3><p>OSHMP30 sets three targets for 2030. Each one counts harm that has already happened: deaths, injuries and work-related illness. Safety professionals call these <i>lagging indicators</i>, because they only tell you about a problem after someone has been hurt.<a href=\"https://dosh.gov.my/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Master-Plan_With-cover-Eng-Version-3.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><sup>1</sup></a></p><p>The targets are written as a rate: how many cases there are for every 1,000 or 100,000 workers. This makes years with different numbers of workers fair to compare. For a starting point, according to interim data from the Department of Occupational Safety and Health (DOSH), reported that in 2024 there were 2.58 injuries for every 1,000 workers and 2.58 deaths for every 100,000 workers. Ten years earlier, in 2014, the figures were 3.10 and 4.21.&nbsp;By 2030, the plan wants that number to be closer to 2.<a href=\"https://www.malaymail.com/news/malaysia/2025/04/28/human-resources-ministry-pursues-zero-vision-as-workplace-injury-rates-drop/174811\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><sup>5</sup></a><br> <br> <img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-safety-box-ready-for-oshmp30/test.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p>Some industries carry far more of the risk. In 2023, 88 of the 324 people who died in work accidents in Malaysia worked in construction, more than in any other industry. Manufacturing came next, with 66 deaths.<a href=\"https://www.dosm.gov.my/portal-main/release-content/big-data-analytics-national-occupational-injury-and-disease-statistics-\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><sup>6</sup></a> That is why OSHMP30 has a priority dedicated to high-risk industries and to micro, small and medium businesses.</p><h3>Measuring before anyone gets hurt</h3><p>The plan also tracks <i>leading indicators</i>. These count what a workplace does to prevent harm, before anyone is hurt. One group of them, called <i>Hazard Prevention and Control</i>, counts \"the number of safety inspections conducted following near-miss reports and the number of corrective measures taken\". A near miss is something that could have hurt someone but, this time, did not. By tracking near misses and what was done about them, the plan says, organisations \"can identify non-compliance issues early\".<a href=\"https://dosh.gov.my/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Master-Plan_With-cover-Eng-Version-3.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><sup>1</sup></a></p><p><b>Example:</b>A pallet falls from a rack and lands a metre from a worker. Nobody is hurt, so no injury is recorded. A good leading indicator still records it, checks the rack and fixes it before the next pallet falls.</p><h2>The plan describes what we build</h2><p>Technology runs all through OSHMP30. The Minister of Human Resources lists \"<em>applying new technology, including AI, big data and automation for OSH</em>\" as one of the ways the plan will be carried out. The chairman of NCOSH names \"<em>mainstreaming technological innovation in risk management in the workplace</em>\" as a key area.<a href=\"https://dosh.gov.my/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Master-Plan_With-cover-Eng-Version-3.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><sup>1</sup></a> Strategic Thrust 3 sets out the goal.</p>\"<em>To promote the integration of innovative technologies and digital solutions in OSH practices to enhance safety monitoring and risk management across various sectors.\"</em><p><cite>OSHMP30, Strategic Thrust 3 objective<a href=\"https://dosh.gov.my/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Master-Plan_With-cover-Eng-Version-3.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><sup>1</sup></a></cite></p><p>Put simply, the plan wants workplaces in every industry to use new technology to keep watch for danger and to manage risk. It points out that today's systems \"often face limitations in integrating and utilising data to effectively analyse and manage risks\". In other words, companies collect information but struggle to use it. It calls for \"integrated monitoring tools and smart data analytics\", meaning tools that keep watch automatically and turn what they see into useful numbers. The thrust has three programmes:<a href=\"https://dosh.gov.my/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Master-Plan_With-cover-Eng-Version-3.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><sup>1</sup></a></p><ul><li><b>Encouraging new safety technology</b>, through innovation labs, safety technology exhibitions, and competitions for universities and businesses.<br> </li><li><b>Helping companies adopt it</b>, through an expert advisory committee, funding to help with the cost, and a <b>DOSH programme to certify and promote safety technology developed in Malaysia</b>.<br> </li><li><b>Improving the government's online safety systems</b>, including OSHConnect, a single online system for safety services, and a directory of safety technology developers and suppliers on MyKKP, DOSH's online portal.</li></ul><p>When the Ministry of Human Resources asked safety technology developers and suppliers to come forward for this thrust, it gave an example of such a developer: a team of programmers building AI software that watches CCTV footage and spots workers who are not wearing safety helmets.<a href=\"https://sites.google.com/mohr.gov.my/rfi-aktiviti2-program3-ts3/home\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><sup>2</sup></a> That is exactly the job we built the IRIV EdgeAI Safety Box to do, all day and all night, on every camera you connect.<br> </p><h2>Meet the IRIV EdgeAI Safety Box</h2><p>The Safety Box is a compact computer that stays at your workplace and connects to the same network as your security cameras or the recorder they already feed into. Inside is the <strong>Cytron IRIV EdgeAI CM5</strong>with a Hailo AI 26TOPS&nbsp;accelerator designed specifically for video-based AI processing.<br> <br> The box analyses camera footage locally to detect safety issues, capture evidence, and generate reports. It does not need to send video over the internet for these tasks. Your video stays at your workplace, while only the alerts and reports you choose to share leave the premises.<br> </p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-safety-box-ready-for-oshmp30/screenshot-2026-10-12-121702.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><section><h3>Safety gear checks</h3><p>Spots anyone without a safety helmet or a high-visibility vest. You choose what each camera checks for, such as both at the site entrance and helmets only in the warehouse.</p> </section><section><h3>No-go areas</h3><p>Mark an area on the camera picture, such as around a machine or a loading bay. The box spots any person or vehicle that goes in: car, lorry, bus, motorcycle or bicycle.</p> </section><section><h3>Photo and video proof</h3><p>Each situation is saved once, with a photo and a short video that shows it from beginning to end.</p> </section><section><h3>Alarms and messages</h3><p>A warning light or siren on site, email or Telegram messages when someone enters a no-go area, and a link to your other site systems.</p> </section><section><h3>Charts and reports</h3><p>See how things change by day, week and month, and get a ready-made Workplace Safety Audit report whenever you need one, or by email every month.</p> </section><section><h3>Private by design</h3><p>Video stays in the box at your workplace. Everyone has their own login, old recordings are deleted on schedule, and backups are password-locked.</p> </section><h3>One record for each situation</h3><p>When a safety rule is broken, the Safety Box records it as an <b>incident</b>. An incident is one situation from start to finish, seen by one camera, for one rule, however many people are involved. The box keeps one photo of it and one short video that starts a few seconds before the problem and ends a few seconds after it, so a supervisor can see what actually happened rather than a single frozen moment. Each individual sighting is kept as well, for anyone who needs the full detail.</p><p><b>Example:</b>Three workers walk past the store camera without helmets, and one of them stops to chat for a minute. That is one incident, with one photo and one video of the whole minute, and not dozens of separate alarms.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-safety-box-ready-for-oshmp30/screenshot-2026-10-12-102655.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p><b>Figure 2. The list of incidents.</b> One line for each situation, showing which camera saw it, what was wrong, how long it lasted, the most people involved at the same time, whether it is still happening, and its photo and video.</p><h3>Warnings that reach the right people</h3><p>When something happens, the Safety Box can warn people in three ways.</p><ul><li><strong>On-site alerts with optional warning lights or sirens.</strong> The box provides two wired output connections for activating external warning lights or sirens: one for missing PPE and one for no-go area violations. Each output can be configured to remain active while the issue persists or trigger a short flash or beep. <strong>Warning lights and sirens are not included with the Safety Box and must be purchased separately.</strong><p><b>Example:</b>A flashing light above the loading bay switches on while someone is standing inside the marked area, so the forklift driver and the worker both know.<br> </p></li><li><b>On your phone, for no-go areas.</b> When someone enters a no-go area, the box can send an email or a Telegram message to supervisors, naming the area and the time. Missing safety gear does not send phone messages. It shows on screen, can switch on the warning light, and appears in the reports.<p><b>Example:</b>At 10:42 pm, a guard's phone receives a Telegram message that a person has entered the chemical store.<br> </p></li><li><b>To your other systems.</b> The box can also pass every incident, for both safety gear and no-go areas, to other software at your workplace, such as a control-room screen or a building management system. It uses MQTT, a common method that industrial equipment uses to share information.</li></ul><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-safety-box-ready-for-oshmp30/screenshot-2026-10-12-104848.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p><b>Figure 3. Connecting lights, sirens and other systems.</b> The settings that pass incidents to other software, and the two wired connections that switch on a warning light or siren, either for as long as the problem lasts or as a short pulse.</p><h2>How the Safety Box supports OSHMP30</h2><p>The Safety Box helps most where the plan asks workplaces to keep watch, collect information and use new technology. The table gives a summary, and the sections after it explain each point.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Thrust</th><th>Fit</th><th>How the Safety Box helps</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>3. New technology</td><td>Core</td><td>AI that watches cameras for danger and turns what it sees into useful numbers, developed by a Malaysian company</td></tr><tr><td>4. Small businesses and high-risk industries</td><td>Core</td><td>Works with the cameras already installed, needs no specialist, and watches for missing helmets and vests and for people entering dangerous areas, all day, every day</td></tr><tr><td>1. Laws, enforcement and self-checking</td><td>Supports</td><td>Dated photo and video proof, and audit reports that show what was watched and what was found</td></tr><tr><td>6. Safety culture and training</td><td>Supports</td><td>Real examples from your own workplace for safety briefings, and charts that show whether things are improving</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Two of the six thrusts are mostly about things a camera cannot see. Thrust 2 is about workers' health. Noise alone caused 82.8% of the work-related illnesses recorded in Malaysia in 2023,<a href=\"https://www.dosm.gov.my/portal-main/release-content/big-data-analytics-national-occupational-injury-and-disease-statistics-\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><sup>6</sup></a> and the thrust also covers chemicals, strain from lifting and awkward postures, and stress. Thrust 5 is about gig work and working from home, away from a fixed workplace. These need other measures, and the Safety Box can work alongside them by passing its incidents to the other systems you use.</p><h3>Thrust 3: the technology the plan asks for</h3><p>The Safety Box turns camera footage you already have into safety information you can use. Instead of relying on someone happening to notice a missing helmet, it watches every connected camera, all the time, and records each lapse with when it happened, where, and a photo and video.</p><p>Its Analytics page turns those records into charts: how many incidents happened, how that compares with the period before, and which areas and which missing items come up most often. That is the \"smart data analytics\" the plan calls for, running at your own workplace.</p><p><b>Example:</b>The chart shows that most missing-helmet incidents happen at Gate 2 just after lunch. The supervisor puts a helmet rack at the gate and holds a short briefing. Next month's chart shows whether it worked.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-safety-box-ready-for-oshmp30/screenshot-2026-10-12-104435.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p><b>Figure 4. Analytics.</b> Safety gear and no-go area incidents by day, week or month, compared with the period before, and broken down by area and by the item that was missing. It counts situations, not every sighting, so one person standing in view for a minute is counted once.</p><p>The plan says companies are held back from adopting safety technology by \"limited awareness, insufficient skills for effective adoption, and funding constraints\".<a href=\"https://dosh.gov.my/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Master-Plan_With-cover-Eng-Version-3.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><sup>1</sup> </a>In plain terms: they don't know about it, don't know how to use it, and worry about the cost.</p><p>We designed the Safety Box to make those easier. It uses the cameras you already have, so you do not need to buy and install a new camera system. Its on-screen setup guide finds those cameras on your network and walks you through the rest step by step, so you do not need a specialist.</p><p>The same thrust introduces a DOSH programme to certify and promote safety technology developed in Malaysia, and an online directory where employers can find safety technology developers and suppliers.<a href=\"https://dosh.gov.my/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Master-Plan_With-cover-Eng-Version-3.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><sup>1</sup></a> Both are good news for locally developed products like the Safety Box, and for the employers looking for them.</p><h3>Thrust 4: built for small businesses and high-risk industries</h3><p>The plan recognises that small businesses often have \"limited financial resources, technical expertise, and awareness of best practices\". In other words, less money, fewer experts and less knowledge of what works. It defines high-risk industries as those with consistently high numbers of deaths, accidents and work-related illness.<br> <br> Its goal is to help them reach \"high levels of OSH compliance through targeted programmes and resources\".<a href=\"https://dosh.gov.my/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Master-Plan_With-cover-Eng-Version-3.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><sup>1</sup></a> RTM's News Portal commentary on the plan points to workers in workshops, furniture making and small contracting firms.<a href=\"https://berita.rtm.gov.my/fokus/perspektif/senarai-berita-fokus/perspektif/senarai-artikel/oshmp30-lindungi-rakyat-yang-keluar-mencari-rezeki/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><sup>8</sup></a></p><p>The Safety Box suits these workplaces for three reasons:</p><ul><li><b>It uses the cameras you already have.</b> Most workplaces already have security cameras. The box watches those, so the main cost is the box itself, not new cameras.<br> </li><li><b>It needs no specialist.</b> The setup guide finds your cameras for you. To mark a no-go area, you click its corners on a photo from the camera. After that, supervisors simply use the Live View, Events and Reports pages.<br> </li><li><b>It watches for the things that lead to serious accidents.</b> According to the Department of Statistics, falls and accidents involving machinery are among the main causes of deaths in construction.<a href=\"https://www.dosm.gov.my/portal-main/release-content/big-data-analytics-national-occupational-injury-and-disease-statistics-\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><sup>6</sup></a> A worker without a helmet, or a person walking into the path of machinery or vehicles, is something the box can see and warn about while there is still time to act.</li></ul><p><br> The thrust also plans regular audits and checks to help businesses \"identify and address workplace risks proactively\", meaning find and fix risks before they cause harm.<a href=\"https://dosh.gov.my/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Master-Plan_With-cover-Eng-Version-3.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><sup>1</sup></a> An inspector or safety officer cannot be on site all the time. The Safety Box keeps watching between their visits.</p><h3>Thrust 1: proof you can rely on</h3><p>Thrust 1 wants companies to take responsibility for checking their own safety, \"driving accountability through effective monitoring\".<a href=\"https://dosh.gov.my/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Master-Plan_With-cover-Eng-Version-3.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><sup>1</sup></a> Its programmes include safety audits based on results, safety records being considered for bank loans and government contracts, safety as part of the environmental, social and governance (ESG) standards that export customers expect, and safety reporting by companies listed on Bursa Malaysia's LEAP Market. All of these need records that others can trust.</p><p>The Safety Box keeps those records. Every incident is dated and timed, with its photo and video. You can search the records and download them as a spreadsheet, or download the photos and videos together in one file with a list of what is in it. The box also produces a printable report called the <b>Workplace Safety Audit</b>. It shows your site and company, the name of the person who prepared it, and whether it is a draft or final version. It also explains how its numbers were counted, so anyone reading it, such as a client, an auditor or a bank, can trace each figure back to the evidence behind it.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-safety-box-ready-for-oshmp30/screenshot-2026-10-12-105338.png\" loading=\"lazy\"><br> <br> <b>Figure 5. The details of one incident.</b> The photo, the video of the whole situation, and each individual sighting with its exact time. Together they give a complete record of what happened.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-safety-box-ready-for-oshmp30/screenshot-2026-10-12-105645.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p><b>Figure 6. Creating a Workplace Safety Audit report.</b> Choose the dates it covers, the site and company, who is preparing it, and whether it is a draft or final version. You can limit it to safety gear or no-go areas, or to particular cameras and areas.</p><p>The law makes good records more important than ever. Malaysia's updated Occupational Safety and Health law took effect on 1 June 2024. Among other changes, employers with five or more workers must now appoint an OSH coordinator, a named person who looks after safety. Workers may now leave a place they believe is in immediate danger. And the highest fine for failing basic safety duties rose tenfold, from RM50,000 to RM500,000.<sup><a href=\"https://www.ukm.my/oshfst/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/A1648-BM.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">7</a></sup> Malaysia has also formally adopted two International Labour Organization agreements on workplace safety (Conventions No. 155 and No. 187).<a href=\"https://dosh.gov.my/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Master-Plan_With-cover-Eng-Version-3.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><sup>1</sup></a> A record of what was watched, what was found and what was done about it helps an employer show that they took reasonable steps to keep people safe. The Safety Box helps you show those steps. The legal duties themselves remain the employer's.<br> </p><h3>Thrust 6: a safety culture built on your own examples</h3><p>Thrust 6 moves away from one-off training courses towards making safety an part of everyday learning and work.&nbsp;It aims for what safety experts call a <i>generative</i> safety culture by 2030: the most mature level, where everyone looks out for safety without being told.<a href=\"https://dosh.gov.my/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Master-Plan_With-cover-Eng-Version-3.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><sup>1</sup></a> Real footage from your own workplace makes powerful training material.<br> </p><p><b>Example:</b>At Monday's five-minute safety briefing, the supervisor shows last week's video of a worker stepping into the forklift lane at the site's own gate. It makes the point far better than a generic slide. The following months' charts then show whether the message got through.</p><h2>Turning everyday lapses into early warnings</h2><p>Each time the Safety Box records a missing helmet or someone entering a no-go area, it has caught an unsafe act before it caused harm. Recorded every time, these incidents are exactly the kind of early warning the plan's <i>Hazard Prevention and Control</i> indicators are meant to capture. They show where lapses keep happening, prompt a check and a fix, and let you see whether the fix worked by comparing one month with the next. That is how a workplace manages its risks before they turn into injuries.</p><h2>Private and secure by design</h2><p>Under Malaysia's Personal Data Protection Act 2010, video in which workers can be recognised counts as personal data. We built the Safety Box to keep that data under your control.</p><ul><li><b>Kept at your workplace.</b> Video is watched and stored inside the box on your site. Nothing leaves it unless you set up alerts or emailed reports.</li><li><b>The right access for each person.</b> Everyone has their own login at one of three levels. A viewer can only look. An operator can also work with the records. An administrator can change settings and manage accounts.</li><li><b>You decide how long to keep things.</b> You choose how many days photos and videos are kept, and older ones are deleted automatically. If the box starts to run out of space, it removes the oldest first so there is always room for new ones.</li><li><b>Password-locked backups.</b> Every backup is locked with a password you choose, so it is useless to anyone who does not know it.</li><li><b>A secure connection.</b> The box's web page uses an encrypted connection.</li></ul><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-safety-box-ready-for-oshmp30/screenshot-2026-10-12-105904.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p><b>Figure 7. Storage settings.</b> How many seconds of video to keep before and after each event, how sightings are grouped into incidents, and how many days to keep evidence before it is deleted automatically.</p><h2>Our advice for getting it right</h2><p>The Safety Box works best as one part of a wider safety plan. Our advice:</p><ul><li><b>Remove the danger first, where you can.</b> Safety experts rank ways of protecting people. The best is to remove the hazard altogether, then to replace it with something safer, then to put up barriers or guards, then to set safe ways of working. Safety gear such as helmets comes last, as the final layer of protection. Use what the Safety Box shows you to decide on those bigger fixes.<p><b>Example:</b>If people keep cutting through the forklift area, a fence or a marked walkway may work better than more alarms.</p></li><li><b>Talk to your workers first.</b> OSHMP30 is built on government, employers and workers working together. Before you switch the cameras on for safety monitoring, explain to your workers and their representatives what is being watched, why, who can see the footage and how long it is kept.<br> </li><li><b>Test it at your site.</b> How well the box sees depends on where the camera is, how far away people are, the lighting and the picture quality. For example, a camera facing a bright doorway may struggle to see a helmet against the glare. Walk in front of each camera without a helmet on purpose to check it responds, and look through its incidents in the first few weeks.<br> </li><li><b>Follow up.</b> The real value comes from what you do about each incident. Give someone, often your OSH coordinator, the job of reviewing incidents and recording what was done.</li></ul><h2>A practical step towards 2030</h2><p>OSHMP30 asks Malaysian workplaces to stop waiting for accidents and start preventing them. It names new technology, better use of information, and support for small businesses and high-risk industries as the way to get there. The Ministry's own example of a safety technology developer is a team building AI that watches CCTV to spot workers without safety helmets.<br> </p><p>The IRIV EdgeAI Safety Box brings that to your workplace today. It is developed in Malaysia by Cytron, works with the cameras most workplaces already have, and keeps your video on your own premises. It helps directly with the plan's priorities on technology and on small businesses and high-risk industries, and supports its priorities on self-checking and safety culture. Used as part of a sound safety plan, and with your workers' trust, it is a practical step towards the plan's 2030 targets.</p><h2>Sources</h2><ol><li>Department of Occupational Safety and Health (DOSH). <i>National Occupational Safety and Health Master Plan 2026–2030 (OSHMP30)</i>, English version. <a href=\"https://dosh.gov.my/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Master-Plan_With-cover-Eng-Version-3.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">dosh.gov.my</a></li><li>Ministry of Human Resources (KESUMA). <i>Programme 3, Strategic Thrust 3, OSHMP30: request for information, OSH technology developers and suppliers</i>. <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/mohr.gov.my/rfi-aktiviti2-program3-ts3/home\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">sites.google.com/mohr.gov.my</a></li><li>Berita RTM. <i>Pelan Induk OSHMP30 fokus pendigitalan, daya tahan sistem keselamatan pekerjaan</i>, 6 November 2025. <a href=\"https://berita.rtm.gov.my/nasional/senarai-berita-nasional/senarai-artikel/pelan-induk-oshmp30-fokus-pendigitalan-daya-tahan-sistem-keselamatan-pekerjaan/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">berita.rtm.gov.my</a></li><li>JKKP / DOSH Malaysia on X. National OSH Week 2026 programme, including the OSHMP30 launch on 28 July 2026. <a href=\"https://x.com/doshmalaysia/status/2076839865560584282\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">x.com/doshmalaysia</a></li><li>Malay Mail. <i>Human Resources Ministry pursues 'Zero Vision' as workplace injury rates drop</i>, 28 April 2025. <a href=\"https://www.malaymail.com/news/malaysia/2025/04/28/human-resources-ministry-pursues-zero-vision-as-workplace-injury-rates-drop/174811\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">malaymail.com</a></li><li>Department of Statistics Malaysia (DOSM). <i>National Occupational Injury and Disease Statistics 2023</i>, released 12 November 2024. <a href=\"https://www.dosm.gov.my/portal-main/release-content/big-data-analytics-national-occupational-injury-and-disease-statistics-\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">dosm.gov.my</a></li><li>Attorney General's Chambers. <i>Occupational Safety and Health (Amendment) Act 2022 (Act A1648)</i>. <a href=\"https://www.ukm.my/oshfst/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/A1648-BM.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">lom.agc.gov.my</a></li><li>Berita RTM, Perspektif. <i>OSHMP30 lindungi rakyat yang keluar mencari rezeki</i>, 30 July 2026. <a href=\"https://berita.rtm.gov.my/fokus/perspektif/senarai-berita-fokus/perspektif/senarai-artikel/oshmp30-lindungi-rakyat-yang-keluar-mencari-rezeki/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">berita.rtm.gov.my</a></li></ol>"}, {"slug": "real-time-ppe-detection-raspberry-pi", "title": "Real-Time PPE Detection on Raspberry Pi With Edge AI", "date": "27 Aug 2026", "author": "Khairul Tajudin", "topic": "ai", "kind": "Project", "desc": "Use Raspberry Pi and an AI accelerator to detect helmets and safety vests at the edge, providing real-time PPE compliance visibility for industrial safety applications.", "cover": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/tutorial/real-time-ppe-detection-raspberry-pi/real-time-ppe-detection-on-raspberry-pi-with-edge-ai-320x180.png", "hero": "https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/real-time-ppe-detection-raspberry-pi/real-time-ppe-detection-on-raspberry-pi-with-edge-ai.png", "tags": ["PPE Detection", "Edge AI", "Raspberry Pi", "AI HAT+", "Computer Vision", "Industrial Safety"], "products": [{"name": "IRIV EdgeAI CM5 - IR4.0 Industrial AI Controller", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/V-IRIV-EAI5/iriv-edgeai-cm5-view-1-1-268x268.png", "price": "RM2,487.00++", "old": null}], "words": 1201, "useCase": false, "body": "<h2>Introduction</h2><p>Personal Protective Equipment (PPE) is one of the most important safety requirements on construction sites, in factories, warehouses, and other hazardous work environments. Helmets, safety vests, and other protective equipment may look simple, but ensuring every worker is properly equipped before entering a restricted area still requires consistent checks.</p><p>This is where edge AI can make the process smarter.</p><p>By combining a camera, Raspberry Pi and a dedicated AI accelerator, PPE compliance can be detected automatically from the camera feed. Instead of checking workers one by one, the system identifies protective equipment such as helmets and safety vests, places bounding boxes around each detection and displays a confidence score together with an overall safety status.</p><p>In one detection scene, multiple workers are analysed at the same time. Helmet and vest detections appear individually while a green <strong>SAFETY OK</strong> indicator provides a simple overall status.</p><p>This turns several individual AI detections into information that can be understood quickly — an important step toward automated PPE monitoring.</p><h2>Why PPE Detection Matters</h2><p>Traditional PPE checking normally depends on supervisors or safety personnel visually inspecting workers at an entrance or around the work area.</p><p>For a small team, that may be manageable. As the number of workers, entrances or monitored areas increases, however, manual checking becomes more difficult to maintain consistently.</p><p>Computer vision provides another approach.</p><p>A camera can monitor a defined work area while an AI model analyses the image for the required PPE classes. Instead of requiring a supervisor to inspect every worker individually, the system can highlight potential compliance conditions automatically and present the information on screen.</p><p>For this project, the main detection targets are <strong>helmets and safety vests</strong>. Gloves are also part of the intended PPE requirement, although glove detection is not included in the demonstrated results.</p><p>The important idea is not simply detecting an object. It is turning visual information from a work area into useful safety information that can eventually become part of a larger industrial safety system.</p><h2>How the PPE Detection System Works</h2><p>The system follows a straightforward edge AI workflow:</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/real-time-ppe-detection-raspberry-pi/blockdiagram-ppe1.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p>A camera first captures the working area. The Raspberry Pi acts as the main computing platform, while the Raspberry Pi AI HAT+ provides dedicated hardware acceleration for the AI workload.</p><p>The detection model analyses each incoming frame and identifies the PPE classes it has been trained to recognise. When an object is detected, the interface draws a bounding box around it and displays the corresponding class together with a confidence score.</p><p>For example:</p><ul><li><strong>Helmet 0.78</strong></li><li><strong>Vest 0.74</strong></li><li><strong>Head 0.47</strong></li></ul><p>These values represent the confidence of each detection. They should not be interpreted as the overall accuracy of the complete PPE detection system.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/real-time-ppe-detection-raspberry-pi/blockdiagram-ppe2.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p>Keeping the AI processing close to the camera is what makes this an <strong>edge AI</strong> application. The image can be analysed locally rather than requiring the core detection process to depend on a remote cloud pipeline.</p><h2>Edge AI Processing with Raspberry Pi AI HAT+</h2><p>At the centre of the AI processing is the <strong>Raspberry Pi AI HAT+ 26 TOPS</strong>, powered by Hailo acceleration technology.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/real-time-ppe-detection-raspberry-pi/hands-holding-raspberry-pi-ai-hat-board.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p>The purpose of the accelerator is straightforward: neural-network inference can be assigned to dedicated AI hardware instead of relying only on the Raspberry Pi's main processor.</p><p>According to the presenter, the PPE detection workload is handled by the AI accelerator rather than the Raspberry Pi CPU. The presenter also describes the detection results as appearing together with the camera feed in real time.</p><p>These points are useful for understanding the intended architecture, although no frame-rate, latency or processor-utilisation measurements are provided.</p><p>From an engineering perspective, using dedicated acceleration is especially useful for computer-vision applications because object detection can become one part of a larger system. The host computer can still be responsible for application logic, visualization and additional integrations while dedicated AI hardware handles the inference workload.</p><h2>PPE Detection Results</h2><p>The PPE detection output demonstrates how multiple objects and multiple workers can be analysed within the same scene.</p><p>In one warehouse scene, a worker wearing a hard hat is detected with:</p><p><strong>Helmet — 0.88</strong></p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/real-time-ppe-detection-raspberry-pi/warehouse-safety-inspection.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p>A second scene contains several workers and produces multiple detections simultaneously. Helmet detections appear in approximately the <strong>0.55 to 0.78</strong> range, while vest detections appear around <strong>0.65 to 0.74</strong>.</p><p>One additional detection is labelled:</p><p><strong>Head — 0.47</strong></p><p>At the same time, the interface displays a green <strong>SAFETY OK</strong> indicator.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/real-time-ppe-detection-raspberry-pi/safety-ok-at-construction-site.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p>This is an important distinction in an AI safety application.</p><p>Individual bounding boxes tell us <strong>what the model detects</strong>, while an overall compliance indicator represents the next layer of application logic — deciding what those detections mean for the safety condition.</p><p>The exact rule behind the <strong>SAFETY OK</strong> indicator is not defined here, so the individual detection results are the clearest part of the implementation to evaluate. Even so, the concept is useful: several object-level detections can eventually be translated into one simple decision for an operator or another control system.</p><h2>From Detection to Industrial Deployment</h2><p>PPE detection is only the first layer of a complete safety-monitoring application.</p><p>The current implementation focuses on:</p><p><strong>Camera input → PPE detection → Confidence score → Compliance visualization</strong></p><p>For a real industrial deployment, the next stage would be deciding what happens after the AI produces that result.</p><p>Depending on the application, the detection status could become an input for a separate integration layer such as event logging, alarms, operator notifications or access-control logic. For example, an entrance-control system could eventually use PPE compliance information as one of the conditions considered before allowing access to a hazardous area.</p><p>Those functions should be treated separately from the detection demonstrated here.</p><p>Glove detection is another possible development area. Helmets, vests and gloves are part of the intended PPE requirement, but the current detection results demonstrate helmet and vest recognition rather than a glove detection class.</p><p>For deployments that need a more industrial platform around this type of edge AI application, Cytron's <strong>IRIV EdgeAI CM5</strong> provides a relevant path forward.</p><p>The IRIV EdgeAI CM5 is an industrial AI controller built around Raspberry Pi Compute Module 5 and supports Raspberry Pi AI acceleration hardware. Cytron also lists <strong>PPE Zone Monitoring</strong> as one of its supported AI vision applications.</p><p>Its industrial interfaces, including isolated digital I/O and isolated RS485, can also support integration with other equipment where additional control or signalling is required.</p><p>The important distinction is that these are capabilities available for building a larger industrial system. The PPE implementation discussed here focuses on the AI detection layer itself.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>Edge AI provides a practical way to bring computer vision directly into workplace safety applications.</p><p>By combining a camera, Raspberry Pi and dedicated AI accelerator, this PPE detection system can identify helmets and safety vests, provide individual confidence scores and present the results as an easy-to-understand compliance status.</p><p>More importantly, the detection result can become the foundation for a much larger safety workflow. PPE recognition can eventually be combined with logging, alerts, operator interfaces or access-control systems depending on the requirements of the deployment.</p><p>For teams moving from a proof-of-concept toward an industrial edge AI system, the <strong>IRIV EdgeAI CM5</strong> provides a platform designed for this type of application, combining Raspberry Pi-based computing, AI acceleration and industrial connectivity in a deployment-ready controller.</p>"}, {"slug": "iriv-edgeai-safety-box-quick-start-guide", "title": "IRIV EdgeAI Safety Box: Quick Start Guide", "date": "17 Aug 2026", "author": "Asri Ruslan", "topic": "ai", "kind": "Guide", "desc": "Official setup instructions for the IRIV EdgeAI Safety Box. Find step-by-step documentation for hardware installation, configuration, and initialization.", "cover": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-safety-box-quick-start-guide/p18-320x180.png", "hero": "https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-safety-box-quick-start-guide/p18.png", "tags": ["IRIV", "Raspberry Pi In Industry", "IRIV EdgeAI"], "products": [{"name": "IRIV EdgeAI - Safety Box: PPE and Zone Monitoring", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edgeai-safety-box-kit", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/KIT-IRIV-EAI-PPE/safety-box-b-268x268.png", "price": "RM7,999.00", "old": null}], "words": 722, "useCase": false, "body": "<h2>What the box does</h2><p><a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edgeai-safety-box-kit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The IRIV EdgeAI Safety Box</a> transforms standard IP CCTV cameras into an intelligent workplace safety system. Using real-time computer vision, it monitors video feeds for Personal Protective Equipment (PPE) compliance and unauthorized access to restricted zones. Upon detecting a violation, the system instantly triggers an alert and automatically logs the event, capturing both snapshot and video evidence for review.<br> </p><p>All inference happens on the device. Video does not leave the site, and the system keeps working when the internet does not.<br> </p><p>Before you start</p><p>First, ensure the IRIV EdgeAI Safety Box is connected to your local network; a wired LAN connection is highly recommended. Once connected, locate the device's IP address displayed on the front OLED screen. Open your web browser and enter this address to access the system interface.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-safety-box-quick-start-guide/p1.jpeg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>First run</h2><p>These steps happen once, in this order, the first time anyone opens a new box.</p><ol><li><h3>Sign in and change the password</h3><p>The factory account is<em>admin</em>&nbsp;with password<em>cytron</em>. The box forces a change on first sign-in. The new password must be at least 8 characters, and cannot be the factory password or the same as the username.<br> <br> <br> <img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-safety-box-quick-start-guide/p9.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p></li><li><h3>Add a camera</h3><p>The setup wizard walks through&nbsp;Secure account → Add camera → Ready. Discovery finds cameras on the network; if they share one set of credentials you can enter it once and pair them together. You can also add an RTSP URL manualy.<br> <br> <img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-safety-box-quick-start-guide/p11.png\" loading=\"lazy\"><br> <br> <br> <br> <img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-safety-box-quick-start-guide/p12.png\" loading=\"lazy\"><br> <br> <img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-safety-box-quick-start-guide/p13.png\" loading=\"lazy\"><br> <br> <img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-safety-box-quick-start-guide/p14.png\" loading=\"lazy\"><br> <br> <img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-safety-box-quick-start-guide/p15.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p></li></ol><p><strong>Later</strong></p><p>The wizard is not one-time-only. It stays available at&nbsp;Settings → Setup wizard&nbsp;if you want to run through it again when adding a second site or a new batch of cameras.<br> <br> </p><h2>Getting around</h2><p>Five tabs across the top, in the order you tend to need them — watching, then understanding, then evidencing, then configuring.<br> <br> <img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-safety-box-quick-start-guide/p16.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><dl><dt><strong>Live View:</strong>Camera grid with detections drawn on the picture as they happen.</dt></dl><dl><dt><strong>Analytics:</strong>Charts and summaries over time.</dt></dl><dl><dt><strong>Events:</strong>The record of what was detected, with evidence.</dt></dl><dl><dt><strong>Reports:</strong>Generate a PDF safety report for a period.</dt></dl><dl><dt><strong>Settings:</strong>Cameras, models, alerts, users, system.</dt></dl><dl></dl><h2>Live View</h2><p>The page a control room leaves open. A tile per camera with detections drawn over the picture as they happen, so what you see marked is what the box acted on. Zones are drawn here too — each on the camera it belongs to.</p><h3><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-safety-box-quick-start-guide/p17.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></h3><h3>Choosing a layout</h3><p>Four arrangements, switched from the toolbar. They change how the tiles are placed, never which cameras are running.</p><table><tbody><tr><td>Auto</td><td>Fits the tiles to the number of cameras and the size of your screen. The right choice unless you want something specific.</td></tr><tr><td>Grid</td><td>Equal tiles, everything the same size. Best for scanning several cameras at once.</td></tr><tr><td>Focus</td><td>One large tile with the rest alongside. For watching one area while keeping half an eye on the others.</td></tr><tr><td>Single</td><td>One camera, as large as the screen allows.</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>The camera list</h3><p>Down the side, listing every camera and whether it is running. Collapse it when you want the width for pictures. This is also where cameras are started and stopped —<strong>Stop</strong>&nbsp;takes a camera out of monitoring entirely, so it produces no detections and no events until it is started again.<strong>Add camera</strong>&nbsp;at the end of the grid goes to where new cameras are set up.</p><h3>Tile controls</h3><table><tbody><tr><td><dl><dt>Full screen</dt></dl></td><td>Expands one tile to fill the display, and takes you back out again.</td></tr><tr><td>Previous / next camera</td><td>Steps through cameras without leaving the layout. The<kbd>←</kbd>&nbsp;and<kbd>→</kbd>&nbsp;arrow keys do the same.</td></tr><tr><td>Camera settings</td><td>Jumps to that camera's configuration rather than making you find it in Settings.</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Smart Live</h3><p>The<strong>Smart</strong>&nbsp;switch in the toolbar. With it on, a tile with nothing moving in front of it stops sending pictures after about thirty seconds and resumes the moment there is motion or a detection. With it off, every running tile streams continuously.</p><h3>The status strip</h3><p>Displays the current violation status and active alert counts, categorized by specific PPE types (e.g., helmets, vests) and active monitoring zones.</p><h3>Recent events</h3><p>A rail of the latest events beside the live picture, so something that just happened is one glance away rather than a page away. Collapse it when you want the room.</p><ul><li>Click a thumbnail to open the snapshot larger.</li><li><kbd>←</kbd>&nbsp;and<kbd>→</kbd>&nbsp;move between snapshots;<kbd>Esc</kbd>&nbsp;closes the preview.</li><li><strong>Open Events</strong>&nbsp;goes to the full record with filters and export.</li></ul>"}, {"slug": "industrial-iot-weather-station-iriv-picontrol", "title": "Industrial IoT Weather Station with Remote Monitoring", "date": "17 Aug 2026", "author": "Khairul Tajudin", "topic": "monitor", "kind": "Project", "desc": "See how IRIV PiControl, solar power, battery monitoring and environmental sensors can turn a basic weather station into a complete remote monitoring node.", "cover": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/tutorial/industrial-iot-weather-station-iriv-picontrol/industrial-iot-weather-station-with-remote-monitoring-320x180.png", "hero": "https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/industrial-iot-weather-station-iriv-picontrol/industrial-iot-weather-station-with-remote-monitoring.png", "tags": ["IRIV PiControl", "Industrial IoT", "Weather Station", "Remote Monitoring", "Environmental Monitoring", "Solar Power"], "products": [{"name": "IRIV PiControl - IR4.0 Industrial Controller", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/V-IRIV-PICONTROL/v-iriv-picontrol-main-3-268x268.jpg", "price": "RM1,489.00++", "old": null}], "words": 1264, "useCase": false, "body": "<h2>Introduction</h2><p>A weather station is usually built to collect environmental information such as temperature, humidity, pressure and air quality. For a simple project, that may be enough. But when the system is installed at an outdoor industrial site or a location that is difficult to access, the challenge becomes much bigger than simply choosing the right sensors.</p><p>The system also needs reliable power, remote access, data monitoring and a way to check whether the station itself is still operating properly. This project demonstrates how an environmental weather station, <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl</a>, solar power and a centralized monitoring dashboard can work together as a complete remote monitoring node.</p><h2>More Than Just a Weather Sensor</h2><p>A basic weather station usually follows a simple flow:<strong>Weather Sensor → Environmental Data</strong></p><p>For remote industrial monitoring, more components are required. The station needs to continuously collect environmental data while operating outdoors with minimal human intervention. In this setup, the weather station is used to monitor parameters such as:</p><ul><li><p>Temperature</p></li><li><p>Humidity</p></li><li><p>Atmospheric pressure</p></li><li><p>Particulate matter</p></li><li><p>Noise level</p></li></ul><p>The data is then handled by the main controller and displayed remotely through a centralized dashboard. However, environmental data is only one part of the system. The monitoring station also needs to know whether its own power source is healthy and whether the device is still online. That is what makes this setup different from a normal weather station.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/industrial-iot-weather-station-iriv-picontrol/weather-station-tower.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>System Overview</h2><p>The system can be divided into three main sections.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Environmental Monitoring</strong> - The outdoor sensor collects weather and environmental conditions around the installation site.</p></li><li><p><strong>Main Controller</strong> - The <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl</a>&nbsp;acts as the main controller, bringing the monitoring data together before it is sent to the dashboard.</p></li><li><p><strong>Power and System Health</strong> - A solar panel, battery and solar charge controller provide power for the remote installation.</p></li></ul><p>A voltage sensor is also used to monitor the battery condition. The overall monitoring flow can be represented as:<strong>Weather Sensor → </strong><a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl</a><strong>&nbsp;→ Remote Dashboard</strong></p><p>While the power system operates as:<strong>Solar Panel → Solar Charge Controller → Battery → Monitoring System</strong></p><p>Battery information can also be monitored:<strong>Battery → Voltage Sensor → </strong><a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl</a><strong> → Dashboard</strong></p><p>This means the system is monitoring two things at the same time:&nbsp;The environment and the condition of the monitoring station itself.</p><p><em><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/industrial-iot-weather-station-iriv-picontrol/system-workflow-weather-station.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></em></p><h2>IRIV PiControl as the Main Controller</h2><p>At the centre of the system is <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl</a>. Instead of treating every sensor and device as an individual system, <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl</a> acts as the main point where data can be collected and processed. For this weather monitoring application, the controller can bring together information such as:</p><ul><li>Environmental readings</li><li>Battery voltage</li><li>Device operating status</li><li>Data required for the monitoring dashboard</li></ul><p>This makes it easier to create a centralized monitoring system instead of checking multiple devices separately. For industrial applications, this becomes useful because a weather station is usually only one part of a larger monitoring system.</p><p><em><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/industrial-iot-weather-station-iriv-picontrol/iriv-picontrol-installed-inside-the-outdoor-electrical-enclosure..jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></em></p><h2>Keeping the System Running Off-Grid</h2><p>One of the biggest challenges with a remote monitoring system is power. If the monitoring station is installed at a location without convenient access to mains electricity, running a new power cable may not be practical. In this project, solar power is used to support the monitoring system.</p><p>The basic power flow is:</p><p><strong>Solar Panel → Solar Charge Controller → Battery → Monitoring System</strong></p><p>The solar panel generates energy during suitable conditions. The solar charge controller manages the charging process, while the battery stores energy that can be used when solar generation is unavailable. This helps the monitoring station operate independently from a nearby mains power source.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/industrial-iot-weather-station-iriv-picontrol/new-weather-station-article-crop.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>Monitoring the Battery Condition</h2><p>Having a battery is useful, but knowing its condition is equally important. For a remote station, a power problem may go unnoticed until the whole system stops sending data. To reduce this risk, the battery voltage is also monitored and displayed on the dashboard. This allows the user to observe how the power system behaves over time.</p><p>For example, the battery voltage trend can help indicate whether the battery is:</p><ul><li>Charging normally</li><li>Maintaining a stable voltage</li><li>Gradually losing charge</li><li>Showing unusual behaviour</li></ul><p>This is an important part of the system because the station does not only monitor the surrounding environment.</p><p><strong>It also monitors its own ability to continue operating.</strong></p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/industrial-iot-weather-station-iriv-picontrol/solar-charge-controller.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>Monitoring Everything from One Dashboard</h2><p>Once the data is collected, it needs to be displayed in a useful way. The monitoring dashboard provides a centralized view of both environmental conditions and system health. The dashboard shown in this project includes information such as:</p><ul><li>Outdoor temperature</li><li>Outdoor humidity</li><li>Atmospheric pressure</li><li>Noise</li><li>PM1.0</li><li>PM2.5</li><li>Battery voltage</li><li>Device status</li></ul><p>Instead of checking multiple devices separately, the user can monitor everything from one place.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/industrial-iot-weather-station-iriv-picontrol/dashboard.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>Viewing Historical Data</h2><p>The dashboard also provides historical trend graphs. This is important because a single sensor value only shows what is happening at that moment. Historical data provides more context.</p><p>For example:</p><ul><li><p>A temperature reading shows the current temperature.</p></li><li><p>A temperature graph shows how the environment has changed throughout the day.</p></li><li><p>The same applies to the battery.</p></li><li><p>A single voltage reading shows the current battery voltage, while a voltage trend makes it easier to understand how the solar charging system is performing.</p></li></ul><p>The project dashboard includes historical information for:</p><ul><li><p>Temperature and humidity</p></li><li><p>Battery voltage</p></li><li><p>Noise</p></li><li><p>Particulate matter</p></li></ul><p>This makes the collected information more useful for monitoring and analysis.</p><h2>Knowing Whether the Station Is Online</h2><p>Another useful feature shown on the dashboard is <strong>Device Status</strong>. For a remote monitoring station, it is important to know whether the system is still connected and communicating. A simple online status indicator can quickly tell the operator whether the monitoring node is still active. When combined with battery voltage monitoring, this provides better visibility into the overall health of the system.</p><p>For example:</p><p><strong>Device Online + Normal Battery Voltage</strong></p><p>suggests that the system is operating normally.</p><p>If the device suddenly goes offline or the battery voltage continuously decreases, further investigation may be required.</p><h2>Why This Matters for Industrial Monitoring</h2><p>A remote weather monitoring station may be useful in many different applications.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Manufacturing Facilities:</strong> Environmental conditions can be monitored around factories, warehouses or production areas.</p></li><li><p><strong>Solar Farms:</strong> Weather information can be collected together with other operational data from the solar site.</p></li><li><p><strong>Agriculture:</strong> Environmental data can support long-term monitoring of conditions around farms, plantations or greenhouses.</p></li><li><p><strong>Remote Equipment Sites:</strong> A self-powered monitoring node can be installed where regular physical inspection is inconvenient.</p></li><li><p><strong>Research and Education:</strong> Environmental conditions can be collected continuously for analysis and long-term studies.</p></li></ul><p>The specific sensors may change depending on the application, but the overall system concept remains similar.</p><h2>From Weather Station to Complete Monitoring Node</h2><p>The main idea behind this project is simple.</p><p>An industrial weather station should not only answer:</p><p><strong>“What is the weather condition?”</strong></p><p>It should also answer:</p><p><strong>“Is the system still operating?”</strong></p><p><strong>“Is the battery healthy?”</strong></p><p><strong>“Can I view the data remotely?”</strong></p><p><strong>“Can I see what happened earlier?”</strong></p><p>That changes the architecture from a simple sensor project into a complete monitoring solution:</p><p><strong>Environmental Sensors → IRIV PiControl → Reliable Power → System Health Monitoring → Remote Dashboard</strong></p><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>Building an industrial IoT weather station involves more than connecting environmental sensors. When the station is installed outdoors or at a remote location, reliable power, system health and remote visibility become just as important as the sensor data itself. By combining environmental sensing, <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl</a>, a solar power system, battery monitoring and a centralized dashboard, this project creates a complete remote monitoring node. The result is a system that monitors not only the surrounding environment, but also whether the monitoring station itself is still healthy and operating.</p>"}, {"slug": "iriv-edgeai-workshop-drb-hicom", "title": "IRIV EdgeAI Workshop with DRB-HICOM", "date": "13 Aug 2026", "author": "Khairul Tajudin", "topic": "events", "kind": "Workshop", "desc": "DRB-HICOM Port Klang joined a two-day IRIV EdgeAI Workshop to explore custom AI model training, logo detection and the potential of Edge AI for industrial product defect detection.", "cover": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-workshop-drb-hicom/iriv-edge-ai-workshop-with-drb-hicom-port-klang-320x180.png", "hero": "https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-workshop-drb-hicom/iriv-edge-ai-workshop-with-drb-hicom-port-klang.png", "tags": ["IRIV EdgeAI", "DRB-HICOM", "Edge AI", "AI Vision", "Defect Detection", "Quality Inspection"], "products": [{"name": "IRIV EdgeAI Industry 4.0 Training: Getting Started with EdgeAI Vision", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/iriv-edge-ai-training-page", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/V-KIT-IRIV-EAI/edge-ai-kit-a-268x268.png", "price": "RM5,000.00", "old": null}], "words": 907, "useCase": false, "body": "<h2>Introduction</h2><p>On <strong>11–12 June 2026</strong>, Cytron conducted a two-day <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/c-workshop-seminar/iriv-edge-ai-training-page\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI Workshop</a> with three participants from <strong>DRB-HICOM Port Klang</strong>.</p><p>The workshop focused on practical Edge AI Vision development, giving participants hands-on experience with the <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI</a> training platform, camera input and custom AI model training.</p><p>One of the main activities was training an AI model to <strong>detect a logo on a shirt</strong>. Instead of only demonstrating a pre-trained AI application, participants experienced how a custom detection model can be prepared, trained and tested using their own target object.</p><p>This exercise also connected directly with DRB-HICOM's intended application: exploring <strong>IRIV EdgeAI for product defect detection</strong> and automated visual inspection.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-workshop-drb-hicom/6249276552575128225.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>Objectives</h2><p>The workshop aimed to introduce participants to the complete AI Vision workflow, from preparing image data and training a model to running AI inference on an Edge AI controller.</p><p>The training also gave the DRB-HICOM team an opportunity to evaluate how the same approach could later be adapted from a simple logo-detection exercise into more practical quality-inspection applications for their own products.</p><h2>Hands-On with IRIV EdgeAI</h2><p>Throughout the workshop, participants worked directly with the <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/c-workshop-seminar/iriv-edge-ai-training-page\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI Industry 4.0 Training Kit</a>, laptops and camera systems.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-workshop-drb-hicom/6249276552575128223.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p>The <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI</a>&nbsp;training platform is based on Raspberry Pi Compute Module 5 and includes a Hailo AI accelerator for running AI workloads locally at the edge. The workshop kit also includes a USB camera and industrial components for hands-on AI and automation exercises.</p><p>Participants were introduced to areas including:</p><ul><li><p>Getting started with <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI</a></p></li><li><p>Node-RED visual programming</p></li><li><p>Camera setup for AI Vision</p></li><li><p>Custom AI dataset preparation</p></li><li><p>AI model training</p></li><li><p>Hailo AI inference using images and video</p></li><li><p>Running a custom-trained AI model</p></li><li><p>Connecting AI results with physical outputs</p></li></ul><p>The official <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/c-workshop-seminar/iriv-edge-ai-training-page\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI Workshop</a> is designed around this combination of custom model training, camera-based inference and industrial integration, allowing participants to move beyond basic AI demonstrations into their own applications.</p><h2>Training AI to Detect a Logo</h2><p>A key hands-on exercise during the workshop was <strong>custom logo detection</strong>.</p><p>Participants used a shirt with a visible logo as the target object for the AI Vision exercise. Images were captured and used as part of the process of preparing a custom dataset and training the AI model.</p><p>Once the model was ready, a camera connected to the system was used to test the detection in a live environment.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-workshop-drb-hicom/6249276552575128219.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p>This exercise helped demonstrate an important concept in industrial AI Vision: an AI system does not have to be limited to generic objects. A model can be trained around specific visual features that are relevant to the intended application.</p><p>The <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/c-workshop-seminar/iriv-edge-ai-training-page\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI Workshop</a> specifically includes custom dataset preparation and inference using custom-trained models as part of its training structure.</p><h2>From Logo Detection to Product Defect Detection</h2><p>While logo detection was used as the workshop exercise, the larger objective for DRB-HICOM was to explore how the same AI Vision approach could be applied to <strong>product defect detection</strong>.</p><p>In a real inspection application, the target could change from identifying a logo to detecting visual conditions such as a missing component, incorrect assembly, surface abnormality or other product characteristics defined by the inspection requirement.</p><p>The overall development process remains similar:</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-workshop-drb-hicom/flow-edgeai-workshop.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p>This is where Edge AI becomes particularly relevant for industrial quality inspection. Instead of sending every image to an external cloud system, AI inference can be performed directly at the edge, close to the camera and production process.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-workshop-drb-hicom/6249276552575128220.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p>Cytron positions the <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI</a> training platform for applications including defect detection, product recognition, object identification and quality inspection.</p><h2>Moving from AI Concept to Industrial Application</h2><p>For companies exploring AI Vision, one of the challenges is moving from a successful AI demonstration to something that can eventually become part of an industrial workflow.</p><p>The workshop gave participants exposure to both sides of that process.</p><p>On the AI side, they worked with image data, custom models and camera inference. On the industrial side, they explored <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI</a> as a controller that can connect AI results with other system functions.</p><p>This makes it possible to expand a vision application beyond simply displaying a detection result. Depending on the final project, an AI result could later be connected to alarms, indicators, dashboards, data recording or other automation processes.</p><p>The <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI</a> training platform supports this combination of AI Vision and industrial integration through its camera interfaces, Node-RED environment and industrial training hardware.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-edgeai-workshop-drb-hicom/6249276552575128222.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>Workshop Outcome</h2><p>By the end of the two-day workshop, the participants had experienced the basic workflow of developing a <strong>custom AI Vision application</strong>, from working with the training kit and camera to preparing and testing a trained model.</p><p>More importantly, the logo-detection exercise gave the team a practical reference for evaluating how <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI</a> could be adapted to their own product-inspection requirements.</p><p>Rather than treating AI as a standalone technology, the workshop demonstrated how AI Vision can become part of a larger industrial system for inspection, monitoring and automation.</p><p>For DRB-HICOM, the next opportunity is to apply the same workflow to actual product images and clearly defined defect conditions, moving from a training exercise towards a practical proof of concept.</p><h2>Community &amp; Next Steps</h2><p>Companies, engineers and system integrators interested in exploring <strong>AI Vision, defect detection or automated quality inspection</strong> can learn more through Cytron's <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/c-workshop-seminar/iriv-edge-ai-training-page\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI Industry 4.0 Training</a> programme.</p><p>The workshop provides hands-on exposure to custom AI model training, camera-based inference and industrial Edge AI implementation.</p><p><strong>IRIV EdgeAI Industry 4.0 Training</strong><br> <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/c-workshop-seminar/iriv-edge-ai-training-page\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://my.cytron.io/c-workshop-seminar/iriv-edge-ai-training-page</a></p><p><strong>Cytron Workshop Landing Page</strong><br> <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/cytron-workshop\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://my.cytron.io/cytron-workshop</a></p>"}, {"slug": "industrial-edge-ai-iriv-edgeai-cm5", "title": "Industrial Edge AI Applications with IRIV EdgeAI CM5", "date": "11 Aug 2026", "author": "Khairul Tajudin", "topic": "ai", "kind": "Insight", "desc": "Explore how IRIV EdgeAI CM5 enables industrial AI applications including defect detection, Data Matrix recognition, safety monitoring, smart CCTV, and vehicle detection.", "cover": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/tutorial/industrial-edge-ai-applications-iriv-edgeai-cm5/industrial-edge-ai-applications-with-iriv-edgeai-cm5-320x180.png", "hero": "https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/industrial-edge-ai-applications-iriv-edgeai-cm5/industrial-edge-ai-applications-with-iriv-edgeai-cm5.png", "tags": ["Edge AI", "Industrial AI", "AI Vision", "IRIV EdgeAI CM5", "Machine Vision", "Smart Factory"], "products": [{"name": "IRIV EdgeAI CM5 - IR4.0 Industrial AI Controller", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/V-IRIV-EAI5/iriv-edgeai-cm5-view-1-1-268x268.png", "price": "RM2,487.00++", "old": null}], "words": 778, "useCase": false, "body": "<h2>Introduction</h2><p>Factories today face many challenges, from product defects and safety risks to inefficient monitoring processes. As manufacturing becomes more connected, AI vision can help automate these tasks and provide faster, more consistent results.</p><p>The <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI CM5</a>&nbsp;is an industrial Edge AI controller designed to run AI vision applications directly at the edge. Instead of relying entirely on cloud processing, AI inference can be performed close to where the data is generated.</p><p>In this article, we explore five industrial AI applications demonstrated using the <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI CM5</a>.</p><h2>1. Real-Time Defect Detection</h2><p>Quality inspection is an important part of any production process.</p><p>Using a camera and an AI vision model, the <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI CM5</a> can inspect products and identify components or abnormalities in real time. In the demonstration, the system monitors a PCB and detects individual components such as capacitors and crystals.</p><p>This approach can help manufacturers identify potential defects earlier in the production line, before they reach the next process or the customer.</p><p><strong>Possible applications include:</strong></p><ul><li><p>PCB component inspection</p></li><li><p>Product presence checking</p></li><li><p>Assembly verification</p></li><li><p>Visual quality inspection</p></li></ul><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/industrial-edge-ai-applications-iriv-edgeai-cm5/pcb-component-inspection-dashboard.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>2. Data Matrix and Barcode Recognition</h2><p>Tracking products throughout the manufacturing process is important for traceability.</p><p>The <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI CM5</a> can also be used to detect and decode <strong>Data Matrix codes</strong> from a camera feed. In the demonstration, multiple Data Matrix codes are detected simultaneously and their information is decoded automatically.</p><p>The system can record information such as the decoded value, detection time, confidence level and location of the detected code.</p><p>This can be useful for applications such as:</p><ul><li><p>Product identification</p></li><li><p>Manufacturing traceability</p></li><li><p>Inventory tracking</p></li><li><p>Serial number verification</p></li><li><p>Production tracking</p></li></ul><p>By automating code recognition, manufacturers can reduce manual scanning while improving traceability across the production line.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/industrial-edge-ai-applications-iriv-edgeai-cm5/data-matrix-detector-dashboard.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>3. Restricted Zone Safety Monitoring</h2><p>AI vision can also be used to improve workplace safety.</p><p>In the safety-monitoring demonstration, a camera monitors a predefined restricted area. When a person enters the monitored zone, the AI system detects their presence automatically.</p><p>This allows industrial facilities to monitor areas where access may be dangerous or restricted.</p><p>For example, the system could be deployed around:</p><ul><li><p>Heavy machinery</p></li><li><p>Robot operating areas</p></li><li><p>Loading zones</p></li><li><p>Restricted factory areas</p></li><li><p>Warehouse safety zones</p></li></ul><p>The detection output could also be integrated with other industrial systems to trigger alarms, warning lights or notifications when required.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/industrial-edge-ai-applications-iriv-edgeai-cm5/baru-safety-monitoring-restricted-zone.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>4. Smart CCTV with Multiple AI Models</h2><p>Traditional CCTV systems usually record video for later review. With Edge AI, the same camera feed can become an active monitoring system.</p><p>The demonstration shows the <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI CM5</a> running <strong>multiple AI models simultaneously</strong>, including:</p><ul><li><p>Face detection</p></li><li><p>Hand detection</p></li><li><p>Person detection</p></li></ul><p>Each camera stream is processed in real time through the Edge AI system.</p><p>This allows existing camera systems to do more than simply record footage. Depending on the AI model used, cameras can become intelligent sensors capable of detecting specific objects, activities or situations.</p><p>Potential applications include factory monitoring, worker detection, security monitoring and operational analytics.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/industrial-edge-ai-applications-iriv-edgeai-cm5/iriv-edgeai-multi-model-dashboard.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>5. Vehicle and Parking Detection</h2><p>Another application demonstrated is AI-based vehicle monitoring.</p><p>Using a fixed camera, the system detects vehicles entering and leaving an area while maintaining vehicle-count information.</p><p>The same concept could be applied to:</p><ul><li><p>Parking occupancy monitoring</p></li><li><p>Vehicle entrance and exit counting</p></li><li><p>Factory vehicle tracking</p></li><li><p>Loading-bay monitoring</p></li><li><p>Traffic-flow analysis</p></li></ul><p>Instead of installing a dedicated sensor at every parking space or entrance, computer vision can use existing camera infrastructure to provide useful vehicle information.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/industrial-edge-ai-applications-iriv-edgeai-cm5/parking-detection-dashboard-and-vehicle-logs.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>One Platform, Multiple Industrial Applications</h2><p>What makes Edge AI useful in industrial environments is its flexibility.</p><p>The same <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI CM5</a> platform can be adapted for different applications simply by changing the camera setup, AI model and software workflow.</p><p>A single platform can therefore support applications ranging from:</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/industrial-edge-ai-applications-iriv-edgeai-cm5/one-platform.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p>This gives system integrators and manufacturers more flexibility when developing AI-powered industrial solutions.</p><h2>Why Edge AI?</h2><p>Running AI directly at the edge means camera data can be processed closer to the factory floor rather than depending entirely on a remote cloud system.</p><p>For industrial applications, this can help enable faster responses and make it easier to integrate AI results with local equipment, dashboards, alarms and other automation systems.</p><p>It also creates opportunities to transform existing cameras into intelligent monitoring tools without requiring every application to use a completely different hardware platform.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>Industrial AI does not have to be limited to a single application.</p><p>From detecting missing components on a production line to monitoring restricted areas, reading Data Matrix codes and counting vehicles, the <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI CM5</a> demonstrates how one Edge AI platform can support many different industrial vision applications.</p><p>For manufacturers and system integrators exploring AI-powered automation, Edge AI provides a practical way to bring intelligent visual monitoring closer to machines, processes and factory operations.</p><p><strong>One Platform. Endless Applications.</strong></p>"}, {"slug": "instant-machine-fault-alerts-iriv-picontrol", "title": "Get Instant Machine Fault Alerts with IRIV PiControl", "date": "10 Aug 2026", "author": "Khairul Tajudin", "topic": "control", "kind": "Project", "desc": "Detect industrial machine faults with IRIV PiControl and Node-RED, then send instant Telegram alerts for faster maintenance response.", "cover": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/tutorial/instant-machine-fault-alerts-iriv-picontrol/get-instant-machine-fault-alerts-with-iriv-picontrol-320x180.png", "hero": "https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/instant-machine-fault-alerts-iriv-picontrol/get-instant-machine-fault-alerts-with-iriv-picontrol.png", "tags": ["IRIV PiControl", "Industrial IoT", "Machine Monitoring", "Node-RED", "Telegram Alert", "Modbus"], "products": [{"name": "IRIV PiControl - IR4.0 Industrial Controller", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/V-IRIV-PICONTROL/v-iriv-picontrol-main-3-268x268.jpg", "price": "RM1,489.00++", "old": null}], "words": 533, "useCase": false, "body": "<h2>Introduction</h2><p>In an industrial environment, every minute of unexpected machine downtime can affect production. While monitoring dashboards can help track machine conditions, operators or maintenance personnel may not always be in front of the screen when a fault occurs.</p><p>By combining <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl</a><strong>, Node-RED, and Telegram notifications</strong>, machine faults can be detected and automatically sent to the responsible personnel for faster response.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/instant-machine-fault-alerts-iriv-picontrol/01-smart-factory-edge-monitor-dashboard.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>From Machine Fault to Instant Notification</h2><p>In this application, <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl</a> acts as an industrial edge controller that connects machine data to the monitoring and notification system.</p><p>Machine conditions such as <strong>temperature, vibration, RPM, load, and pressure</strong> can be monitored through a centralized dashboard. When abnormal conditions are detected, the system identifies the affected machine and generates an alert.</p><p>For example, the monitoring dashboard detects a fault on <strong>CNC-MILL-01</strong>, showing conditions such as high temperature and excessive vibration.</p><p>Instead of relying on someone to continuously watch the dashboard, the fault information can be processed automatically and delivered directly to the responsible person.</p><p><strong>System Flow:</strong></p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/instant-machine-fault-alerts-iriv-picontrol/system-workflow.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>Making Industrial Errors Easier to Understand</h2><p>Node-RED is used to process incoming machine or communication errors before sending the notification.</p><p>For Modbus communication, technical errors can also be converted into simpler messages. For example:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Timed out</strong> → No response from slave</p></li><li><p><strong>State sending error</strong> → Communication failure while writing</p></li><li><p><strong>CRC error</strong> → Data corruption detected</p></li></ul><p>This makes the notification easier for operators and maintenance personnel to understand without having to interpret the raw system error.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/instant-machine-fault-alerts-iriv-picontrol/02-nodered-modbus-error-processing.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>Alert the Right Person, Wherever They Are</h2><p>Once a fault is detected, the system automatically sends an alert to Telegram.</p><p>The notification can include information such as:</p><ul><li><p>Affected machine or production line</p></li><li><p>Type of fault</p></li><li><p>Error details</p></li><li><p>Required action</p></li></ul><p>For example, if a conveyor stops unexpectedly, the responsible PIC can receive an <strong>“Attention Required”</strong> notification directly on their smartphone and respond immediately.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/instant-machine-fault-alerts-iriv-picontrol/new-telegram-machine-fault-alert.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p>This reduces the need for constant manual monitoring and helps maintenance teams react faster when a problem occurs.</p><h2>Industrial Edge Monitoring with IRIV PiControl</h2><p>Installed directly inside the industrial control panel, <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl</a> provides the interface between industrial equipment and edge applications.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/instant-machine-fault-alerts-iriv-picontrol/04-iriv-picontrol-control-panel.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p>With industrial connectivity such as <strong>RS485</strong> and support for software platforms such as <strong>Node-RED</strong>, it can be used to collect machine information, process data locally, and integrate it with monitoring dashboards or notification services.</p><p>This makes it suitable for applications such as:</p><ul><li><p>Machine condition monitoring</p></li><li><p>Production line fault alerts</p></li><li><p>Equipment downtime notification</p></li><li><p>Remote maintenance alerts</p></li><li><p>Industrial data acquisition</p></li><li><p>Modbus device monitoring</p></li></ul><h2>Faster Awareness, Faster Response</h2><p>A monitoring dashboard provides visibility, but combining it with automatic notifications makes the system more practical for daily industrial operations. With <strong>IRIV PiControl and Node-RED</strong>, machine faults can be detected at the edge and automatically delivered to the people who need to take action.</p><p>The result is a simple industrial monitoring approach that helps maintenance teams become aware of problems sooner, respond faster, and reduce the impact of unexpected machine downtime.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>With <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl</a>, Node-RED, and Telegram notifications, machine faults can be detected and delivered directly to the responsible personnel without relying on continuous dashboard monitoring. This helps maintenance teams respond faster, improve fault visibility, and reduce the impact of unexpected machine downtime.</p><p><a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/IRIV-PiControl\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Explore IRIV PiControl</a></p>"}, {"slug": "iriv-picontrol-workshop-va-integrated", "title": "IRIV PiControl Workshop with VA Integrated Corporate Sdn Bhd", "date": "07 Aug 2026", "author": "Khairul Tajudin", "topic": "events", "kind": "Workshop", "desc": "VA Integrated joined a two-day IRIV PiControl workshop at Cytron HQ to explore Node-RED, industrial control and Industry 4.0 solutions for future customer projects.", "cover": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/tutorial/iriv-picontrol-workshop-va-integrated/iriv-picontrol-workshop-with-va-integrated-corporate-320x180.png", "hero": "https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-picontrol-workshop-va-integrated/iriv-picontrol-workshop-with-va-integrated-corporate.png", "tags": ["IRIV PiControl", "VA Integrated", "Industry 4.0", "Node-RED", "Industrial Automation", "Digitalisation"], "products": [{"name": "IRIV PiControl Industry 4.0 Workshop and Training Bundles", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-industry-4p0-kit-and-workshops", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/V-KIT-IRIV/iriv-training-kit-new-main-268x268.png", "price": "RM2,880.15++", "old": null}], "words": 475, "useCase": false, "body": "<h2>Introduction</h2><p>On <strong>4–5 June 2026</strong>, Cytron conducted a two-day <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-industry-4p0-kit-and-workshops\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl Workshop</a> with <strong>VA Integrated Corporate Sdn Bhd</strong> at <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Cytron Headquarters</a>.</p><p>As a solution provider helping companies digitalise their operations, VA Integrated explored how <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl</a> can support the development of Industry 4.0 solutions for its customers.</p><p>The workshop combined industrial hardware with Node-RED visual programming, allowing participants to experience how control logic, physical input and output, and browser-based dashboards can work together in one system.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-picontrol-workshop-va-integrated/6249276552575128217.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>Hands-On with IRIV PiControl</h2><p>Throughout the workshop, participants worked directly with the<a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-industry-4p0-kit-and-workshops\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl Industry 4.0 Training Kit.</a></p><p>They connected the industrial training panels to their laptops, inspected the hardware setup and explored how the Raspberry Pi-powered controller communicates with industrial components.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-picontrol-workshop-va-integrated/6249276552575128214.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p>The workshop covered areas including:</p><ul><li><p>Getting started with IRIV PiControl</p></li><li><p>Node-RED visual programming</p></li><li><p>Industrial input and output control</p></li><li><p>Relay and push-button testing</p></li><li><p>Browser-based dashboards</p></li><li><p>Hardware and software integration</p></li><li><p>Basic system testing and troubleshooting</p></li></ul><p>This hands-on approach allowed participants to understand the complete workflow rather than learning the hardware and software separately.</p><h2>Developing Control Logic with Node-RED</h2><p>Node-RED was one of the main tools used throughout the training.</p><p>Participants built visual programming flows and connected them to the physical components inside the <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl</a> panel. They could then test how commands from the software affected the industrial hardware.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-picontrol-workshop-va-integrated/6249276552575128212.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p>The workshop demonstrated a simple connected workflow:</p><p><strong>Node-RED flow → IRIV PiControl → Industrial input and output → Dashboard and physical response</strong></p><p>Because Node-RED uses a visual flow-based interface, participants could develop, test and modify their control logic more quickly.</p><h2>Supporting Customer Digitalisation Projects</h2><p>For a solution provider, understanding both software and industrial hardware is important when developing a complete customer solution.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-picontrol-workshop-va-integrated/6249276552575128209.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p><a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl</a> provides a platform for connecting sensors, machines, control components and dashboards using an industrial Raspberry Pi system.</p><p>Depending on the customer’s requirements, the platform can support solutions involving:</p><ul><li><p>Machine and equipment monitoring</p></li><li><p>Data collection and visualisation</p></li><li><p>Industrial automation</p></li><li><p>Alert and notification systems</p></li><li><p>Remote monitoring dashboards</p></li><li><p>Industrial IoT applications</p></li></ul><p>The workshop gave VA Integrated practical exposure to how these elements can be combined when developing future Industry 4.0 projects.</p><h2>Workshop Outcome</h2><p>By the end of the workshop, participants had gained practical experience with <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl</a>, Node-RED, industrial input and output, and dashboard development.</p><p>The training provided a foundation for VA Integrated to further explore how the platform can be adapted for its own customer requirements and Industry 4.0 solutions.</p><p>More than learning how to operate a controller, the workshop focused on understanding how hardware, software and data can be integrated into a complete digitalisation project.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-picontrol-workshop-va-integrated/6249276552575128202.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>Companies and solution providers interested in developing industrial automation, monitoring or digitalisation projects can explore Cytron’s <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl</a> workshops.</p><p>The training can be adapted according to the participants’ experience, business requirements and intended industrial applications.</p><p><strong>Cytron Workshop Landing Page</strong><br> <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/cytron-workshop\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://my.cytron.io/cytron-workshop</a></p><p><strong>IRIV PiControl Industry 4.0 Kit and Workshop</strong><br> <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/c-workshop-seminar/p-iriv-picontrol-industry-4p0-kit-and-workshops\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://my.cytron.io/c-workshop-seminar/p-iriv-picontrol-industry-4p0-kit-and-workshops</a></p>"}, {"slug": "industrial-energy-monitoring-iriv-picontrol", "title": "Real-Time Industrial Energy Monitoring with IRIV PiControl", "date": "06 Aug 2026", "author": "Khairul Tajudin", "topic": "monitor", "kind": "Project", "desc": "See how IRIV PiControl helps factories monitor machine electrical data in real time and support earlier maintenance.", "cover": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/tutorial/industrial-energy-monitoring-iriv-picontrol/real-time-industrial-energy-monitoring-with-iriv-picontrol-320x180.png", "hero": "https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/industrial-energy-monitoring-iriv-picontrol/real-time-industrial-energy-monitoring-with-iriv-picontrol.png", "tags": ["IRIV PiControl", "Industrial Energy Monitoring", "Smart Factory", "Node-RED", "Grafana", "IRIV Pi Control"], "products": [{"name": "IRIV PiControl - IR4.0 Industrial Controller", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/V-IRIV-PICONTROL/v-iriv-picontrol-main-3-268x268.jpg", "price": "RM1,489.00++", "old": null}], "words": 688, "useCase": false, "body": "<h2>Overview</h2><p>Monitoring the electrical condition of industrial machines is important for maintaining stable production. Changes in current, voltage or power may indicate that a machine is operating differently from its normal condition. However, some factories still rely on technicians to inspect these readings manually.</p><p>In this application, <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl</a> is used as the main controller for a real-time industrial energy monitoring system. It collects electrical data from a production machine and displays the readings through a monitoring dashboard.</p><p>This gives technicians better visibility into machine behaviour and helps them respond earlier when unusual readings appear.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/industrial-energy-monitoring-iriv-picontrol/energy-1.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>From Manual Checks to Real-Time Monitoring</h2><p>Manual inspection only provides information at the moment the machine is checked. Changes that happen between inspections may not be noticed immediately.</p><p>With a connected monitoring system, <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl</a> continuously collects available electrical measurements while the machine is operating.</p><p>The demonstrated system monitors:</p><ul><li><p>Voltage</p></li><li><p>Line voltage</p></li><li><p>Current</p></li><li><p>Total power</p></li><li><p>Power factor</p></li><li><p>Frequency</p></li></ul><p>These readings can be viewed through a central dashboard without repeatedly checking the measuring device at the machine.</p><h2>How the System Works</h2><p><a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl</a> acts as the main controller, or the “brain,” of the monitoring system.</p><p>It receives electrical measurements from the connected meter or sensor and transfers the data to the monitoring platform. The system uses:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Node-RED</strong> to manage the data flow</p></li><li><p><strong>Grafana</strong> to display live readings and historical trends</p></li><li><p><strong>Cloudflare</strong> as part of the network-access setup</p></li></ul><p>The exact electrical meter, communication protocol and Cloudflare configuration are not explained in the video.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/industrial-energy-monitoring-iriv-picontrol/energy-4.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>Viewing Machine Data on the Dashboard</h2><p>It includes live gauges and a historical line graph, allowing technicians to see both the current readings and how the machine’s electrical behaviour changes over time.</p><p>During the demonstration, the dashboard shows approximately:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Demonstration Reading</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Average voltage</td><td>81–82 V</td></tr><tr><td>Average line voltage</td><td>162–164 V</td></tr><tr><td>Current</td><td>5.8–6.9 A</td></tr><tr><td>Total power</td><td>449–541 W</td></tr><tr><td>Power factor</td><td>0.93–0.96</td></tr><tr><td>Frequency</td><td>49.8–50.2 Hz</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These are readings from the demonstration machine and are not <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl</a> product specifications.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/industrial-energy-monitoring-iriv-picontrol/energy-3.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>Detecting Abnormal Machine Behaviour</h2><p>Electrical readings can provide useful information about machine operation.</p><p>For example, an unexpected change in current may indicate that the machine load has changed. Technicians can compare the live reading with historical data to determine whether the change is part of normal production or requires further inspection.</p><p>The system may help identify:</p><ul><li><p>Unexpected current changes</p></li><li><p>Irregular power consumption</p></li><li><p>Voltage variation</p></li><li><p>Changes in power factor</p></li><li><p>Unusual operating patterns</p></li></ul><p>Alerts can also be configured when selected values move outside suitable operating limits. However, the alert method and threshold settings are not shown in the video.</p><p>These limits should be based on the machine’s normal operating condition and the manufacturer’s recommendations.</p><h2>Supporting Earlier Maintenance</h2><p>The main purpose of the system is not only to display machine data. It is to provide information that supports maintenance decisions.</p><p>When an abnormal pattern appears, technicians can review when the change started, compare it with previous readings and inspect the machine before the problem becomes more serious.</p><p>This can help maintenance teams move from reactive checks towards a more informed maintenance approach.</p><h2>Benefits for Industrial Operations</h2><ul><li><p><strong>Better Machine Visibility:</strong> Technicians can view important electrical readings through one dashboard.</p></li><li><p><strong>Earlier Problem Detection:</strong> Unusual electrical changes can be noticed before they develop into larger machine issues.</p></li><li><p><strong>Historical Data Analysis:</strong> Recorded trends provide a clearer understanding of machine behaviour over time.</p></li><li><p><strong>More Efficient Maintenance:</strong> Maintenance decisions can be supported by actual machine data instead of manual checks alone.</p></li><li><p><strong>Foundation for Smart Factory Applications:</strong> The same system can later be expanded to include more machines, sensors, dashboards and alert devices.</p></li></ul><h2>Conclusion</h2><p><a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl</a> provides a practical platform for real-time industrial energy monitoring.</p><p>By collecting machine electrical data and displaying it through Node-RED and Grafana, the system gives technicians a clearer view of machine behaviour during operation.</p><p>Live readings and historical trends can help users identify unusual changes, plan maintenance earlier and reduce reliance on manual monitoring.</p><p>For factories beginning their smart-factory journey, industrial energy monitoring is a useful starting point for turning machine data into actionable information.</p><h2>Build a Smarter Industrial Monitoring System</h2><p>Use <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl</a> to collect machine data, create real-time dashboards and develop connected industrial applications.</p><p><a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/IRIV-PiControl\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Explore IRIV PiControl</a></p>"}, {"slug": "ai-packaging-detection-missing-item-inspection", "title": "AI Packaging Detection for Missing Item Inspection", "date": "04 Aug 2026", "author": "Khairul Tajudin", "topic": "ai", "kind": "Project", "desc": "Discover how IRIV EdgeAI CM5 can power an AI vision system that detects missing items and verifies packages before shipment.", "cover": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/tutorial/ai-packaging-detection-missing-item-inspection/ai-packaging-detection-for-missing-item-inspection-320x180.png", "hero": "https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/ai-packaging-detection-missing-item-inspection/ai-packaging-detection-for-missing-item-inspection.png", "tags": ["IRIV EdgeAI CM5", "AI Packaging Detection", "Missing Item Detection", "Computer Vision", "Industrial AI", "Quality Inspection"], "products": [{"name": "IRIV EdgeAI CM5 - IR4.0 Industrial AI Controller", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/V-IRIV-EAI5/iriv-edgeai-cm5-view-1-1-268x268.png", "price": "RM2,487.00++", "old": null}], "words": 871, "useCase": false, "body": "<h2>Overview</h2><p>Packaging errors can happen when operators need to place several items into the same box. A small component may be overlooked before the package is sealed and sent to the customer.</p><p>This project demonstrates an AI packaging inspection system powered by the <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI CM5</a>. An overhead camera monitors the packing area while the controller processes the camera feed, detects the required items and updates the inspection dashboard.</p><p>The system acts as an additional quality-control checkpoint before shipment.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/ai-packaging-detection-missing-item-inspection/1.5.1.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>Why Use IRIV EdgeAI CM5?</h2><p>The <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI CM5</a> is an industrial AI controller based on the Raspberry Pi Compute Module 5. It is designed for edge AI, computer vision and industrial automation applications.</p><p>For this packaging inspection application, the controller can process the camera feed locally and run the AI detection model without depending on continuous cloud processing. This helps reduce latency and allows the system to respond immediately when an item is detected or missing.</p><p>The controller supports AI accelerators with up to <strong>26 TOPS</strong>, allowing it to run object detection, classification and other computer-vision workloads at the edge. It also provides USB 3.0 and dual MIPI CSI camera interfaces for connecting industrial or embedded cameras.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/ai-packaging-detection-missing-item-inspection/1.4.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>System Overview</h2><p>The system consists of:</p><ul><li><p>An overhead camera monitoring the packaging area</p></li><li><p><a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI CM5</a> running the AI detection application</p></li><li><p>A monitoring dashboard showing the inspection status</p></li><li><p>A predefined checklist for each package</p></li><li><p>Optional industrial indicators or alarms</p></li></ul><p>The basic workflow is:</p><p><strong>Camera → IRIV EdgeAI CM5 → AI item detection → Packaging checklist → PASS or warning</strong></p><p>The <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI CM5</a> can also interface with external equipment through its isolated digital inputs, digital outputs and RS485 connection. This makes it possible to integrate the inspection result with signal towers, alarms, PLCs or other factory equipment.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/ai-packaging-detection-missing-item-inspection/cytron-packaging-system.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>How the Packaging Inspection Works</h2><p>The operator starts by selecting the required package configuration. Once the inspection session begins, the overhead camera continuously captures the packaging area. The camera feed is sent to the <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI CM5</a>, where the AI model identifies the items placed inside the box.</p><p>Each detected item is automatically marked on the dashboard checklist. Items that have not yet been detected remain highlighted, allowing the operator to see the package status in real time.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/ai-packaging-detection-missing-item-inspection/1.1.1.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>Complete Package Detection</h2><p>Once the box is closed, the system performs a final verification. When every required item has been detected, the dashboard displays a <strong>PASS</strong> result. This confirms that the package is complete and may proceed to the next stage.</p><p>The system also updates the confirmed package counter, helping operators track completed inspections.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/ai-packaging-detection-missing-item-inspection/1.3.1.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>Missing Item Detection</h2><p>If the package is closed while an item is still missing, the system displays an incomplete-package warning. The dashboard identifies the missing component and instructs the operator to reopen the box, insert the item and close the package again.</p><p>The package is only approved after the checklist is complete.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/ai-packaging-detection-missing-item-inspection/1.2.1.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>Results</h2><p>The demonstration shows that the system can support both complete and incomplete package inspections. During a complete inspection, the required items are detected and the package receives a PASS result. During an incomplete inspection, the system prevents the package from being approved and identifies the missing item for the operator.</p><p>Running the detection directly on the <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI CM5</a> provides fast local processing while keeping the system suitable for factory-floor deployment. Its fanless metal enclosure, DIN-rail mounting support and 10–30V DC power input make it more suitable for industrial installations than a standard development board.</p><h2>Benefits</h2><p>Using <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI CM5</a> for packaging inspection can help businesses:</p><ul><li><p>Reduce missing-item errors</p></li><li><p>Provide immediate operator feedback</p></li><li><p>Perform AI processing locally</p></li><li><p>Reduce reliance on cloud connectivity</p></li><li><p>Prevent incomplete packages from proceeding</p></li><li><p>Standardise packaging inspections</p></li><li><p>Integrate the result with industrial alarms or equipment</p></li><li><p>Improve traceability and packing accuracy</p></li><li><p>Reduce customer complaints and replacement costs</p></li></ul><h2>Other Possible Applications</h2><p>The same <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI CM5</a> platform can also support:</p><ul><li><p>Product counting</p></li><li><p>Defect detection</p></li><li><p>Assembly verification</p></li><li><p>Label inspection</p></li><li><p>Barcode or Data Matrix reading</p></li><li><p>Sorting applications</p></li><li><p>PPE monitoring</p></li><li><p>Restricted-zone monitoring</p></li><li><p>E-commerce fulfilment inspection</p></li></ul><p>Its camera interfaces, AI acceleration support and industrial I/O make it suitable for computer-vision systems that need to detect an event and trigger a physical response.</p><h2>Related Product</h2><p>The <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI CM5</a> is a compact industrial AI controller designed for computer vision, industrial automation and edge analytics.</p><p>Key features relevant to this project include:</p><ul><li><p>Raspberry Pi Compute Module 5</p></li><li><p>Up to 16GB RAM</p></li><li><p>Up to 26 TOPS AI acceleration</p></li><li><p>Two MIPI CSI camera interfaces</p></li><li><p>Two USB 3.0 ports</p></li><li><p>Dual Gigabit Ethernet</p></li><li><p>Isolated digital inputs and outputs</p></li><li><p>Isolated RS485</p></li><li><p>10–30V DC surge-protected input</p></li><li><p>Fanless metal enclosure</p></li><li><p>DIN-rail mounting support</p></li><li><p>M.2 NVMe storage expansion</p></li></ul><h2>Related Tutorials</h2><ul><li><p><a href=\"design-h-tutorial.html?slug=getting-started-with-iriv-edgeai-cm5\">Getting Started with IRIV EdgeAI CM5</a></p></li><li><p><a href=\"design-h-tutorial.html?slug=custom-object-detection-on-iriv-edge-ai\">Custom Object Detection on IRIV EdgeAI</a></p></li><li><p><a href=\"design-h-tutorial.html?slug=industrial-edge-ai-applications-iriv-edgeai-cm5\">Industrial Edge AI Applications</a></p></li><li><p><a href=\"design-h-tutorial.html?slug=enhance-rtsp-camera-with-iriv-edge-ai\">IRIV EdgeAI CM5 for Intelligent CCTV Applications</a></p></li></ul><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>This project demonstrates how the <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI CM5</a> can be used as the core controller for an AI packaging inspection system.</p><p>By processing the camera feed locally, detecting the required items and verifying the package before shipment, the system helps reduce human error and improve packaging consistency.</p><p>The industrial design and connectivity of the <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-edge-ai-cm5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV EdgeAI CM5</a> also allow the solution to be expanded beyond dashboard monitoring, including integration with alarms, signal towers, PLCs and other factory equipment.</p>"}, {"slug": "iriv-picontrol-worldskills-tvet-mara", "title": "IRIV PiControl for WorldSkills at TVET MARA Sungai Petani", "date": "31 Jul 2026", "author": "Khairul Tajudin", "topic": "events", "kind": "Workshop", "desc": "TVET MARA Sungai Petani explored IRIV PiControl and Node-RED as a practical platform for preparing innovation projects for WorldSkills.", "cover": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/tutorial/iriv-picontrol-worldskills-tvet-mara/iriv-picontrol-workshop-at-tvet-mara-sungai-petani-320x180.png", "hero": "https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-picontrol-worldskills-tvet-mara/iriv-picontrol-workshop-at-tvet-mara-sungai-petani.png", "tags": ["IRIV PiControl", "TVET MARA", "Sungai Petani", "WorldSkills", "Node-RED", "Industry 4.0"], "products": [{"name": "IRIV PiControl Industry 4.0 Workshop and Training Bundles", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-industry-4p0-kit-and-workshops", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/V-KIT-IRIV/iriv-training-kit-new-main-268x268.png", "price": "RM2,880.15++", "old": null}], "words": 471, "useCase": false, "body": "<h2>Introduction</h2><p>On <strong>18–19 May 2026</strong>, Cytron conducted a two-day <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-industry-4p0-kit-and-workshops\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl Workshop</a> at <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/TVETMARASungaiPetani/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TVET MARA Sungai Petani</a>.</p><p>The workshop focused on using <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl</a> and Node-RED to support the institution’s preparation for the <strong>WorldSkills Innovation category</strong>. Participants worked directly with industrial training panels, laptops and browser-based control interfaces to understand how software logic can interact with physical hardware.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-picontrol-worldskills-tvet-mara/6249276552575128187.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>Hands-On with IRIV PiControl</h2><p>During the workshop, participants connected their laptops to the IRIV PiControl training panels and tested the system through guided hands-on activities.</p><p>They explored the industrial panel components, physical push buttons, input and output connections, and browser-based interfaces. This allowed them to observe how commands created in the software could control and monitor the connected hardware.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-picontrol-worldskills-tvet-mara/6249276552575128189.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p>The activities introduced participants to:</p><ul><li>IRIV PiControl and Raspberry Pi CM4</li><li>Node-RED visual programming</li><li>GPIO and relay control</li><li>Browser-based control interfaces</li><li>Industrial hardware integration</li><li>Basic system testing and troubleshooting</li></ul><h2>Building Control Logic with Node-RED</h2><p>Node-RED was one of the main tools used during the workshop.</p><p>Participants developed visual programming flows by connecting functional nodes on their laptops. These flows were then linked to the IRIV PiControl panel to control physical components such as push buttons and relays.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-picontrol-worldskills-tvet-mara/6249276552575128192.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p>The visual programming approach makes it easier to understand the relationship between:</p><p><strong>Node-RED flow → IRIV PiControl → Industrial input and output → Physical response</strong></p><p>This gives participants a faster way to test ideas and improve their system logic without developing every function from the beginning.</p><h2>Preparing for WorldSkills Innovation</h2><p>For the WorldSkills Innovation category, participants need more than a project idea. They must also demonstrate how the proposed system works and how it can address a practical problem.</p><p>IRIV PiControl provides a suitable platform for this preparation because it combines industrial hardware with the flexibility of Raspberry Pi and Node-RED.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-picontrol-worldskills-tvet-mara/6249276552575128184.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p>The platform can support prototype ideas involving automation, equipment monitoring, safety alerts, environmental data, dashboards and connected industrial systems.</p><p>During the workshop, participants experienced a basic project-development process:</p><p><strong>Develop the logic → Connect the hardware → Build the interface → Test the system → Improve the prototype</strong></p><h2>Workshop Outcome</h2><p>By the end of the workshop, participants had gained practical exposure to Node-RED, industrial input and output control, browser-based interfaces and IRIV PiControl hardware.</p><p>The workshop provided TVET MARA Sungai Petani with a foundation for continuing its WorldSkills Innovation preparation using an industrial Raspberry Pi platform.</p><p>The goal was not only to complete the workshop exercises, but also to help the team understand how IRIV PiControl can be adapted for future competition prototypes and technical training.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-picontrol-worldskills-tvet-mara/6249276552575128183.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>Training institutions and competition teams interested in developing industrial automation or innovation projects can explore Cytron’s IRIV PiControl workshops.</p><p>The training can be adapted according to the participants’ experience, project requirements and competition objectives.</p><p><strong>Cytron Workshop Landing Page</strong><br> <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/cytron-workshop\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://my.cytron.io/cytron-workshop</a></p><p><strong>IRIV PiControl Industry 4.0 Kit and Workshop</strong><br> <a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/c-workshop-seminar/p-iriv-picontrol-industry-4p0-kit-and-workshops\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">https://my.cytron.io/c-workshop-seminar/p-iriv-picontrol-industry-4p0-kit-and-workshops</a></p>"}, {"slug": "iriv-pi-control-the-key-to-enhanced-industrial-efficiency", "title": "IRIV PiControl: The Key to Enhanced Industrial Efficiency", "date": "18 Sep 2023", "author": "Abdulrahman Alhamed", "topic": "control", "kind": "Use case", "desc": "Efficiency translates to profitability, and one of the essential metrics used to gauge it is Overall Equipment Effectiveness (OEE)", "cover": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/tutorial/iriv-picontrol-the-key-to-enhanced-industrial-efficiency/thumbnail-320x180.png", "hero": "https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-picontrol-the-key-to-enhanced-industrial-efficiency/thumbnail.png", "tags": ["Raspberry Pi"], "products": [{"name": "IRIV PiControl - IR4.0 Industrial Controller", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/V-IRIV-PICONTROL/v-iriv-picontrol-main-3-268x268.jpg", "price": "RM1,489.00++", "old": null}], "words": 411, "useCase": true, "body": "<h2>Introduction</h2><p>In the fast-paced world of industrial operations, one constant remains: the pursuit of excellence. Efficiency translates to profitability, and one of the essential metrics used to gauge it is Overall Equipment Effectiveness (OEE). OEE measures how effectively manufacturing equipment is utilised in a given time frame, encompassing factors like availability, performance, and quality. In this article, we'll explore how the IRIV PiControl is making a significant impact, maximising OEE, and making efficiency and productivity more attainable for all.</p><h2>The Challenge</h2><p>Imagine a bustling factory with numerous machines working in tandem. But, like any real workplace, challenges arise that can disrupt smooth operations. Let's look at three common problems:</p><ul><li><strong>Elevated Temperatures</strong>: Machines often generate heat; sometimes, this heat can become excessive, causing potential problems for both productivity and safety.<br> </li><li><strong>High Humidity Levels</strong>: Maintaining the right humidity levels is crucial in many manufacturing processes. High humidity can lead to product defects and equipment corrosion.<br> </li><li><strong>Unusual Vibrations</strong>: Machines naturally vibrate, but sometimes these vibrations become irregular and unusual. These irregularities may signal impending machine breakdowns or the need for immediate maintenance.</li></ul><p>These challenges, if left unaddressed, can lead to downtime, decreased production efficiency, and increased operational costs.</p><h2>The Solution</h2><p>IRIV PiControl Controller serves as your versatile solution. It seamlessly integrates into your industrial setup, addressing essential functions such as temperature regulation, vibration management, and humidity control through external sensors. These sensors diligently watch over your operations, promptly notifying you of any irregularities and enabling you to take preventive measures.<br> </p><p>What sets the IRIV PiControl apart is its remote monitoring capability and real-time data access. It empowers you to supervise operations from any location, make informed decisions in real time, and collect valuable data. This data can be seamlessly sent to the cloud, ensuring continuous operational efficiency, exceptional product excellence, and access to valuable insights.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/iriv-picontrol-the-key-to-enhanced-industrial-efficiency/diagram.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><h2>Why IRIV PiControl?</h2><p>OEE is a critical metric in manufacturing. It tells us how well machines are working and helps boost production, minimise downtime, and improve product quality, all of which are vital for success in the industry.<br> </p><p>The IRIV PiControl is your all-in-one solution for industrial efficiency. It empowers proactive problem-solving by offering quick access to critical data and remote control capabilities, which significantly reduces unplanned downtime. Furthermore, its real-time monitoring capabilities maintain consistent product quality, resulting in fewer defects and less rework. Additionally, by optimising equipment efficiency, the IRIV PiControl helps your business not only meet production targets but also achieve higher overall productivity.</p>"}, {"slug": "solar-farm-management-with-raspberry-pi", "title": "Solar Farm Management with Raspberry Pi", "date": "15 Oct 2023", "author": "Abdulrahman Alhamed", "topic": "monitor", "kind": "Use case", "desc": "This article explores how the combination of Raspberry Pi and IRIV PiControl is transforming the landscape of solar farm oversight", "cover": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/tutorial/solar-farm-management-with-raspberry-pi/industrial-thumbnail-9-320x180.png", "hero": "https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/solar-farm-management-with-raspberry-pi/industrial-thumbnail-9.png", "tags": ["Raspberry Pi in Industry"], "products": [{"name": "IRIV PiControl - IR4.0 Industrial Controller", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/V-IRIV-PICONTROL/v-iriv-picontrol-main-3-268x268.jpg", "price": "RM1,489.00++", "old": null}, {"name": "Raspberry Pi 5 Single Board Computer", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-raspberry-pi-5", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/V-RASPBERRY-PI-5/v-raspberry-pi-5-main-268x268.jpg", "price": "RM202.00++", "old": null}], "words": 458, "useCase": true, "body": "<h2>Introduction</h2><p>Efficiency and reliability are the foundations of successful solar farm management. This article examines how the integration of Raspberry Pi and IRIV PiControl is revolutionising the landscape of solar farm oversight. We'll explore the cost-effective and dependable solutions these technologies offer, highlighting their pivotal role in streamlining and optimising solar farm management for a sustainable energy future.</p><h2>The Power of Raspberry Pi in Solar Farm Management</h2><p>Raspberry Pi is a versatile and cost-effective single-board computer that has found applications in various industries, including renewable energy. For solar farm management, its affordability and adaptability make it an excellent choice. The IRIV controller, in particular, stands out for its remarkable features, which are tailored to meet the needs of solar farms.</p><h3>IRIV PiControl: A Solar Farm Solution</h3><p>The IRIV controller can be used to centralise the efficient management of solar farms. One of its key advantages is the readily available RS485 and isolated IO interfaces. These interfaces simplify the connection of various components, including inverters and monitoring devices. Here's how it can transform solar farm management:</p><ul><li><strong>Cost-Effectiveness</strong>: Raspberry Pi computers are renowned for their affordability, and IRIV PiControl capitalises on this aspect. By integrating Raspberry Pi, you can significantly reduce hardware costs while maintaining high performance.<br> </li><li><strong>Reliability</strong>: Solar farm management systems require consistent and reliable performance. The IRIV PiControl powered by Raspberry Pi CM4 is designed for robustness and reliability, it offers a dependable solution that minimizes downtime and ensures continuous operation.<br> </li><li><strong>Integration</strong>: The RS485 interface enables seamless integration with inverters and other essential components. This ensures that data exchange between devices is efficient and reliable. This integration eliminates the need for expensive and proprietary hardware, streamlining the system.<br> </li><li><strong>Isolated IO Interfaces</strong>: Isolated IO interfaces ensure that the system can handle data from various sources without interference. This feature enhances data accuracy and security, two vital aspects of solar farm management.</li></ul><h2>Centralised Performance Monitoring</h2><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/solar-farm-management-with-raspberry-pi/diagram2.png\" loading=\"lazy\"></p><p>One of the most significant advantages of integrating Raspberry Pi and IRIV PiControl into your solar farm management system is the centralisation of performance monitoring. Multiple inverters or panels can be conveniently monitored through a single dashboard. This simplifies performance tracking for solar farms with multiple units, offering real-time insights into energy production, system health, and potential issues. Such centralised monitoring streamlines maintenance efforts, reduces operational costs, and ensures optimal energy production.</p><h3>Summary</h3><p>Incorporating Raspberry Pi computers, especially with the advanced features of the IRIV PiControl, presents a cost-effective and reliable solution for efficient solar farm management. The integration of readily available RS485 and isolated IO interfaces simplifies system connectivity, ensuring consistent and accurate data exchange. With a centralised dashboard for monitoring and management, solar farms can operate at peak efficiency and meet the growing demands for clean energy production.</p>&nbsp;"}, {"slug": "factory-power-monitoring-with-raspberry-pi", "title": "Factory Power Monitoring with Raspberry Pi", "date": "02 Oct 2023", "author": "Abdulrahman Alhamed", "topic": "monitor", "kind": "Use case", "desc": "Efficient power management is a fundamental concern in today's industrial landscape. Factories and manufacturing facilities, typically large consumers of energy, are constantly on the lookout for", "cover": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/tutorial/factory-power-monitoring-with-raspberry-pi/thumbail-320x180.png", "hero": "https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/factory-power-monitoring-with-raspberry-pi/thumbail.png", "tags": ["Raspberry Pi", "Raspberry Pi in Industry"], "products": [{"name": "IRIV PiControl - IR4.0 Industrial Controller", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/V-IRIV-PICONTROL/v-iriv-picontrol-main-3-268x268.jpg", "price": "RM1,489.00++", "old": null}, {"name": "Raspberry Pi 5 Single Board Computer", "url": "https://my.cytron.io/p-raspberry-pi-5", "img": "https://static.cytron.io/image/cache/catalog/products/V-RASPBERRY-PI-5/v-raspberry-pi-5-main-268x268.jpg", "price": "RM202.00++", "old": null}], "words": 499, "useCase": true, "body": "<h2>Introduction</h2><p>Efficient power management is a fundamental concern in today's industrial landscape. Factories and manufacturing facilities, typically large consumers of energy, are constantly on the lookout for practical ways to optimise their power consumption. The Raspberry Pi computers offer a customizable and cost-effective solution. This article examines how the Raspberry Pi Compute Module 4 streamlines power monitoring in factories, providing a straightforward and accessible solution.</p><h2>Why Monitor Power Consumption?</h2><ul><li><strong>Understanding Operational Costs</strong><br> </li></ul><p>Imagine a business owner who wants to know how much certain machines are costing them in electricity bills every month. Without proper monitoring, it's challenging to determine which machines are contributing to increased energy costs. Regular utility bills provide a total cost but lack the details needed for specific insights.<br> </p><p>Power consumption monitoring is not just about tracking the total energy used; it's about understanding how each machine contributes to operational costs. Take, for instance, a reflow oven in a manufacturing facility. It may run continuously, consuming energy even during idle periods, which could be a substantial waste.<br> </p><p>When you set up a power monitoring system, you can track how each machine uses energy in real-time. This data provides a clear picture of energy usage patterns and identifies areas for improvement.<br> </p><ul><li><strong>Early Issue Detection</strong><br> </li></ul><p>Monitoring power consumption isn't just about knowing how much energy is used. It's also like having a warning system for your machines.<br> </p><p>For example, if one machine suddenly starts using significantly more power, it could indicate a problem. If you don't act quickly, this problem may worsen, leading to downtime, expensive repairs, and higher energy bills.<br> </p><p>With real-time power data from the monitoring system, factory managers can spot these unusual changes early. They can resolve the issue before it becomes a major problem, saving both time and money while maintaining efficient energy use.</p><h2>Implementing Power Monitoring with IRIV PiControl</h2><p><a href=\"https://my.cytron.io/p-iriv-picontrol-cm4-industrial-controller\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IRIV PiControl</a>, powered by the Raspberry Pi Compute Module 4 (CM4), stands as a great solution for efficient power consumption monitoring in industrial settings. This industrial controller offers a range of features and capabilities that make power monitoring accessible and effective.</p><p><img alt=\"\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://static.cytron.io/image/tutorial/factory-power-monitoring-with-raspberry-pi/diagram.png\" loading=\"lazy\"><img alt=\"\"><img alt=\"\"><img alt=\"\"><img alt=\"\"></p><p>To monitor power consumption effectively with IRIV PiControl, the process is streamlined and straightforward:</p><ol><li>Power monitoring begins by integrating CT (Current Transformer) sensors onto the machines of interest. These sensors collect precise data on energy consumption.<br> </li><li>The collected data is then transmitted to IRIV PiControl, serving as a central hub for organised data management and processing.<br> </li><li>IRIV PiControl takes the processed data and seamlessly sends it to the cloud for remote monitoring. This cloud-based accessibility allows for convenient oversight of energy consumption from any location, enhancing accessibility and convenience.<br> </li></ol><p>One notable feature of IRIV PiControl is its scalability, accommodating data <strong>from up to 31 machines</strong> when using the MODBUS RTU over RS485 interface. This comprehensive approach to power monitoring streamlines the process, providing valuable insights into energy consumption without the need for complex installations.<br> </p><p>IRIV PiControl with CM4 streamlines industrial power monitoring, reducing costs and enabling the timely detection of issues.</p>&nbsp;"}];
